#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --no-project --python >=3.12 python
"""scripts/release — land-to-prod in one command: cut at main SHA S, wait for CI, deploy.

`/go` lands its change to main (`scripts/ship --quick`), then runs this. It
freezes S (default: origin/main) as a release branch, waits for GitHub CI on
exactly that head, and deploys it. Later pushes to main never affect it.

All lifecycle logic stays in `scripts/round` (open, close, cut, gate, deploy
and their guards); this is a thin orchestrator that calls it as a subprocess
and never bypasses a refusal.

  scripts/release [--sha S] [--ci-timeout MIN]
                  [--poll SEC] [--dry-run] [--adopt]
                  [--cut-only | --deploy-only r<N>]

--cut-only (the dark release pass's `release_cut` op): steps 1-5 only. After
the cut (or on finding this worktree's own release already cut) it prints
`RELEASE_CUT: r<N> <head sha>` and `RELEASE_RANGE: <prev_prod>..<head>` and
exits 0 without waiting for check.yml; a release in flight elsewhere, or S
already live, prints the usual line and no RELEASE_CUT. Resuming this
worktree's own release that went red while its head is fully on main
supersedes it in the record and re-cuts at S — a dead cut never reports.
--deploy-only r<N>
(the `release_deploy` op): never cuts; refuses (exit 1) unless release/r<N>
is the live recorded release and this worktree owns it (--adopt takes it),
waits for CI only if the head is not yet green, then runs step 7 and prints
`RELEASE_DEPLOYED: r<N> <head> <prev_prod or ->` after the rollout. A deploy
leg that ran and exited non-zero marks the record `failed` (via
`scripts/round fail`) — terminal while its journal shows `scripts/deploy`
never launched, so the next cut supersedes it; a launched rollout's
`failed` record stays a live partial rollout the leg resumes. A refusal
before any work — no deployment journal — never marks it, and neither does
`scripts/deploy` refusing ahead of its reachability ping, before any rollout
step (exit 75, passed through): `scripts/round deploy` has put the record
back, so the release stays live and a later --deploy-only r<N> deploys it.

Steps, in order:
  1. fetch origin main/prod; S = --sha or origin/main; S must sit on
     origin/main's first-parent line (else exit 1).
  2. S already in origin/prod: the release owner can finish its matching open
     round without another rollout; siblings and --cut-only exit 0, "already live".
  3. A release in flight (a live round.json record or an unrecorded origin
     `release/*`; a branch recorded terminal, or whose head origin/main
     already contains, is a leftover — ignored, with the delete command
     printed for a human; the script never deletes a remote branch. An
     unrecorded branch whose head holds S is in flight even on main:
     round.json is per checkout, so it is another checkout's cut of S, and
     cutting S again would gate it twice, gr646224):
     - this worktree owns the record (`.release-owner` marker at the worktree
       root, written at cut, matching the recorded branch + base; round.json
       is shared by every worktree, so "recorded" is not "mine"): resume
       (6-7). `--adopt` takes ownership of a recorded release whose cutting
       session died (prints a warning);
     - the open release's (or the deploying one's, or an unrecorded
       branch's) head contains S: "riding release ...", exit 0;
     - otherwise exit 0: S "rides the next cut". No queueing (release-train
       §5): the next cut is made at deploy start by the driver. When every
       in-flight record is stale (a deploying/deployed record `round
       supersede --dry-run` would accept — origin/prod already moved past
       its pin) there is no live owner to ride: exit 4 naming the record
       and `scripts/round supersede r<N>`; a stale record shares the next
       deploy's journal path, so it is a blocker, not a ride.
  4. Round: open one if none; close + reopen one that already deployed.
  5. `round cut --sha S` (its stdout, incl. `late:` and `fleet say`, passes
     through). Losing a race to a sibling cut goes back to 3.
  6. Poll `round gate --json` until the candidate equals the release head.
     Early red comes from the head's check-runs (sha-keyed REST `gh api`, never
     `gh run list`: stale cache, see scripts/last-gated-main-sha): a completed
     `lint` / `failed-first` / `test-linux*` / `gate-fast*` run that is not
     success/skipped/neutral exits 2
     with job names, URLs and the `scripts/ci-failed` line (`soak*` legs are
     non-blocking — a completed red one warns and is recorded in prod as
     `release.soak_red`, the debt `round cut`/`open` read on the last gated
     main sha). The release
     branch stays; fix via `scripts/ship --release` or `round cut --abandon`.
     --ci-timeout also exits 2. Either verdict (green or red, with the run id)
     is recorded in prod before any deploy as a row of the `release.ci_verdicts`
     setting, `{sha, range: '<prev_prod>..<sha>', verdict, run_id, at}`
     (best-effort, through scripts/prod-precis); the plan gate `tests-green`
     reads it first (src/precis/plans/gates.py).
  7. `round deploy`, output streamed. Exit 3 (rollout done, runtime pending)
     prints the `next:` confirm line and exits 3. The deploy playbook's last
     gate (deploy/redeploy-precis.yml, "Verify every managed precis venv")
     fails the run unless the running precis-web reports the pinned sha in
     /healthz's X-Precis-Boot-Sha header, so a web left on the previous build
     (gr496849) is a failed deploy, not a green one. After a rollout (exit 0
     or 3) the deployed head is recorded in prod as the `release.prod_head`
     setting, `{head, deployed_at, landed}` (best-effort, through
     scripts/prod-precis); the plan gates `sha-in-prod` / `tests-green` read
     it (src/precis/plans/gates.py). Then scripts/gripe-validate closes
     eligible verify gripes whose resolved sha is now on origin/prod.
     A live-when:<service> gripe waits for a successful lookup showing a
     positive service_config priority anywhere (gr541800; best-effort).

Re-running after any partial state resumes: a recorded release containing S
skips the cut (owner only; the marker is removed after `round close`
succeeds or once the recorded release is gone; a re-run retries a close that
failed); a journal with rollout done prints the `next:` line at once.
A converged deployment with an unpublished prod pointer takes `round deploy
--finish-pointer`, using round's receipt proof without repeating the rollout.

Declared commit pairings (gr687874) use `Change-Set: <id> [<n>]` and
`Requires: <sha>` trailers (preserved verbatim by ship, gr517803).
The target is checked over origin/main's first-parent line
only where a cut is selected: a re-run resumes or reports a recorded
release at its own base even when the requested tip straddles an
incomplete set, a pending cut that would still push reconciles at its
recorded base after the same check (a base that would split a set holds
until the pending record is abandoned), and a pending cut whose branch
origin already holds at its base is finalized unchecked (nothing new is
published; `round cut --require-pushed` refuses if the branch is gone by
then). The checked origin/main snapshot is passed to `round cut
--main-at`, which pushes nothing when its own refresh of main differs: the
fresh cut re-checks and retries (holding once the retry cap is spent), the
other cut paths hold. A cut below the tip that would split a landed set
holds with a `holding: ` line (a classified wait, like in-flight); an
incomplete Change-Set (fewer than n members, those on origin/prod counted
as shipped) moves an older hand-pinned cut below its oldest landed member.
Tip and factory cuts hold for completion. After 24 hours from the oldest
unshipped member's committer timestamp, missing members warn and cease
blocking the cut. A Requires value naming no commit on origin/main is
unenforceable: the cut warns on stderr, naming
the commit and the value, and does not hold on it.

--dry-run only reads; it prints the plan and passes --dry-run to round cut and
round deploy where they apply.

Exit codes: 0 live / riding / next-cut / done; 1 refused; 2 CI red or
timeout; 3 runtime confirmation pending on a live rollout; 4 the only
in-flight record is stale (origin/prod moved past it — this worktree's
own record too; clear with `round supersede`); any other =
`round deploy`'s.

PRECIS_ROUND_ROOT selects the checkout (as for scripts/round).

Catpath wheel preflight relief (gr584832): the deploy's preflight
(scripts/deploy, gr263082) dies without a controller-side autocatpath wheel
source, which the fix-lane host can never have (catpath is private). When
uv.lock's autocatpath pin has no checkout here AND every
autocatpath_plugin host's wheelhouse already holds the pin-matching wheel,
this script hands `round deploy` PRECIS_DEPLOY_SKIP_CATPATH_WHEEL=1 — the
seed is already done. Anything unproven (a pin below pyproject's floor —
the gr499104 guard the bypass would skip — a mismatch, an unreadable
inventory, an unreachable host) changes nothing: the preflight still
fails loudly.

Factory overlay gate (gr700433): the controller's 20k play provisions the
fix-lane inventory as a Git clone with a deploy-helper remote and a sibling
vault pass. Under PRECIS_RELEASE_PASS, cut and deploy require that layout
so the deploy's overlay-sync preflight can reconcile the pinned render's
inventory and vault. Refusal exits 75 with "no host was touched", the
pre-rollout status release_deploy.poll reads.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import math
import os
import re
import runpy
import shlex
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from collections.abc import Callable
from datetime import UTC, datetime
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
_CI = runpy.run_path(str(HERE / "lib/ci_run.py"))
_NETWORK = runpy.run_path(str(HERE / "lib/git_network.py"))
ROUND = HERE / "round"
OK_CONCLUSIONS = ("success", "skipped", "neutral")
OPEN_STATES = ("cut", "gating", "fixing", "green")
DEPLOYING_STATES = ("deploying", "deployed")
#: `scripts/deploy`'s exit code for a refusal before any rollout step, as
#: `scripts/round deploy` passes it on (gr730609); a test pins the copies.
DEPLOY_RC_UNTOUCHED = 75
# Terminality is a predicate, not a set (gr593418 review): `failed` counts
# terminal only while its journal shows `scripts/deploy` never launched —
# no `started_epoch`, no `rollout_at`. A failed record whose rollout
# launched is a live partial rollout.
_is_terminal = runpy.run_path(str(HERE / "lib" / "round_state.py"))["is_terminal"]
_JQ = (
    '.check_runs[] | [.name, .status, (.conclusion // ""), .html_url,'
    ' (.details_url // ""), .id] | @tsv'
)
MAX_CUT_RACES = 5


def _root() -> Path:
    override = os.environ.get("PRECIS_ROUND_ROOT", "").strip()
    return Path(override) if override else HERE.parent


ROOT = _root()


def say(msg: str) -> None:
    print(msg, flush=True)


def _error_detail(exc: BaseException) -> str:
    """TimeoutExpired's string omits captured stderr; retain it explicitly."""
    detail = f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}"
    err = getattr(exc, "stderr", None)
    if isinstance(err, bytes):
        err = err.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    if err:
        detail += f"\n{err.strip()}"
    return _NETWORK["redact"](detail)


def _command_failed(command: str, cp: subprocess.CompletedProcess[str]) -> None:
    detail = cp.stderr.strip() or "command returned no error text"
    redact = _NETWORK["redact"]
    print(
        redact(f"{command} failed (exit {cp.returncode}): {detail}"),
        file=sys.stderr,
    )


def git(*args: str) -> str | None:
    if args and args[0] in ("push", "fetch", "ls-remote"):
        runner = _NETWORK["run_git"]
        cp = runner(list(args), cwd=ROOT, timeout=120)
        return cp.stdout.strip() if cp.returncode == 0 else None
    try:
        cp = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", str(ROOT), *args],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            check=False,
            timeout=120,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        print(f"git {shlex.join(args)}: {_error_detail(exc)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return None
    if cp.returncode and (args[0] != "rev-parse" or cp.stderr):
        _command_failed(f"git {shlex.join(args)}", cp)
    return cp.stdout.strip() if cp.returncode == 0 else None


def ancestor(older: str, newer: str) -> bool:
    cp = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", str(ROOT), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", older, newer],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        encoding="utf-8",
        check=False,
    )
    if cp.returncode not in (0, 1):
        _command_failed(f"git merge-base --is-ancestor {older} {newer}", cp)
    return cp.returncode == 0


def rev(name: str) -> str:
    return git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{name}^{{commit}}") or ""


def remote(ref: str) -> str | None:
    """None = unreadable, empty = absent."""
    out = git("ls-remote", "origin", ref)
    if out is None:
        return None
    return next((ln.split()[0] for ln in out.splitlines() if ln.split()[1] == ref), "")


def release_branches() -> list[str] | None:
    out = git("ls-remote", "--heads", "origin", "release/*")
    if out is None:
        return None
    return [ln.split("refs/heads/", 1)[1] for ln in out.splitlines() if "\trefs/" in ln]


def rnd(
    *args: str, stream: bool = False, env: dict[str, str] | None = None
) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess[str]:
    argv = [str(ROUND), *args]
    if stream:
        return subprocess.run(
            argv, cwd=ROOT, check=False, text=True, encoding="utf-8", env=env
        )
    return subprocess.run(
        argv,
        cwd=ROOT,
        check=False,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        encoding="utf-8",
        env=env,
    )


def die(code: int, msg: str) -> int:
    print(msg, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
    return code


def fetch() -> None:
    if git("fetch", "-q", "origin", "main", "prod") is not None:
        return
    for ref in ("main", "prod"):  # a ref absent on origin fails the combined fetch
        git("fetch", "-q", "origin", ref)


def round_state() -> dict[str, Any] | None:
    cp = rnd("status", "--json")
    if cp.returncode:
        _command_failed("scripts/round status --json", cp)
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(cp.stdout)
        cur = data["round"]
    except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as exc:
        print(
            f"scripts/round status --json unreadable: {_error_detail(exc)}",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return None
    return cur if cp.returncode == 0 and isinstance(cur, dict) else None


def next_round_n() -> int | None:
    """The N the next `round cut` mints (status --json's top-level next_n).

    Numbers are global on origin (gr573151); None when unreadable, so callers
    fall back to the local record's arithmetic.
    """
    cp = rnd("status", "--json")
    if cp.returncode:
        _command_failed("scripts/round status --json", cp)
        return None
    try:
        n = json.loads(cp.stdout).get("next_n")
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return n if type(n) is int else None


def head_containing(sha: str, branch: str, base: str) -> tuple[str, bool]:
    """(head or base of a release, whether it contains sha)."""
    head = remote(f"refs/heads/{branch}") or ""
    if head and not rev(head):
        git("fetch", "-q", "origin", f"refs/heads/{branch}")
    tip = head or base
    return tip, bool(tip and rev(tip) and ancestor(sha, tip))


def _run_id(row: list[str]) -> int:
    try:
        return int(row[5])
    except (IndexError, ValueError):
        return 0


def _row_run_id(row: list[str]) -> int:
    """Workflow-run id a check-run row belongs to, from its job urls."""
    for u in row[3:5]:
        m = re.search(r"/actions/runs/(\d+)", u)
        if m:
            return int(m.group(1))
    return 0


def _check_run_rows(head: str) -> tuple[list[list[str]], int] | bool:
    """(check-run rows scoped to the current run, its run id); False = gh
    unreadable.

    gr504329: check-runs are sha-keyed, so rows span every workflow run
    ever made on this sha — a cancelled job from a superseded run
    poisoned a fresh release's verdict while the new run had not yet
    produced its own check-run for that name (r84, 2026-10-22). Scope
    the verdict to the CURRENT run: the newest check.yml run id
    `gh run list` reports, else the newest run id the rows' urls carry.
    A job name absent from the current run is pending, not red. A row
    with no parseable run id stays — a red we cannot date is still a
    red worth seeing.
    """
    try:
        cp = subprocess.run(
            [
                "gh",
                "api",
                f"repos/{{owner}}/{{repo}}/commits/{head}/check-runs",
                "--paginate",
                "--jq",
                _JQ,
            ],
            cwd=ROOT,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            check=False,
            timeout=120,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        print(f"gh api check-runs for {head}: {_error_detail(exc)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return False
    if cp.returncode != 0:
        print(
            f"gh api check-runs for {head} failed (exit {cp.returncode}): {cp.stderr.strip()}",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return False
    rows = [ln.split("\t") for ln in cp.stdout.splitlines() if ln.count("\t") >= 3]
    current_run = 0
    rid = _regate_run_id(head)
    if rid.isdigit():
        current_run = int(rid)
    if not current_run:
        current_run = max((_row_run_id(r) for r in rows), default=0)
    if current_run:
        rows = [r for r in rows if _row_run_id(r) in (0, current_run)]
    return rows, current_run


def _latest_checks(rows: list[list[str]], keep: Any) -> list[list[str]]:
    """The latest completed check per name in the requested leg family."""
    latest: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    for r in rows:
        if not keep(r[0]):
            continue
        old = latest.get(r[0])
        if old is None or _run_id(r) >= _run_id(old):
            latest[r[0]] = r
    return [r for r in latest.values() if r[1] == "completed"]


def _bad_verdict(bad: list[list[str]]) -> dict[str, Any]:
    urls: list[str] = []
    for r in bad:
        for u in (r[3], r[4] if len(r) > 4 else ""):
            if u and u not in urls:
                urls.append(u)
    run_id = ""
    for u in urls:
        m = re.search(r"/actions/runs/(\d+)", u)
        if m:
            run_id = m.group(1)
            break
    return {"jobs": [r[0] for r in bad], "urls": urls, "run_id": run_id}


def _blocking_leg(name: str) -> bool:
    """A verdict leg: gate-fast legs block on the new shape (me580555);
    test-linux stays blocking for runs cut before the soak rename, and
    failed-first counts even though every shard it gated then reads
    `skipped` (release train §3). `soak*` is deliberately NOT blocking."""
    return (
        name in ("lint", "failed-first")
        or name.startswith("test-linux")
        or name.startswith("gate-fast")
    )


_STOPPED_CONCLUSIONS = frozenset({"cancelled", "timed_out"})
_OVER_BUDGET = "over budget (no verdict)"


def ci_red(head: str) -> dict[str, Any] | bool | str | None:
    """The red verdict, a stopped-run report, False if unreadable, or None."""
    scoped = _check_run_rows(head)
    if scoped is False:
        return False
    rows, current_run = scoped
    checks = _latest_checks(rows, _blocking_leg)
    stopped = [r[0] for r in checks if r[2] in _STOPPED_CONCLUSIONS]
    if stopped:
        return f"CI {_OVER_BUDGET}: {', '.join(stopped)}"
    terminal = completed_run_red(str(current_run)) if current_run else None
    if isinstance(terminal, (dict, str)):
        return terminal
    bad = [r for r in checks if r[2] == "failure"]
    return _bad_verdict(bad) if bad else terminal


def soak_red(head: str) -> dict[str, Any] | bool | None:
    """False = gh unreadable; None = no completed red `soak*` leg on the
    current run; else the failing soak jobs (non-blocking — the
    ``continue-on-error`` legs leave the run conclusion green, so this is
    the only place their red is surfaced)."""
    scoped = _check_run_rows(head)
    if scoped is False:
        return False
    rows, _run = scoped
    verdict_job = runpy.run_path(str(HERE / "lib/soak_verdict.py"))["VERDICT_JOB"]
    checks = _latest_checks(
        rows, lambda name: name.startswith("soak") or name == verdict_job
    )
    bad = [r for r in checks if r[2] not in OK_CONCLUSIONS]
    return _bad_verdict(bad) if bad else None


def completed_run_red(run_id: str) -> dict[str, Any] | bool | str | None:
    """Classify a completed workflow and its blocking jobs."""
    try:
        cp = subprocess.run(
            ["gh", "run", "view", run_id, "--json", "status,conclusion,jobs,url"],
            cwd=ROOT,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            check=False,
            timeout=120,
        )
        if cp.returncode:
            raise RuntimeError(f"exit {cp.returncode}: {cp.stderr.strip()}")
        run = json.loads(cp.stdout)
        if not isinstance(run, dict):
            raise ValueError("expected a workflow-run object")
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, RuntimeError, ValueError) as exc:
        print(f"gh run view {run_id} unreadable: {_error_detail(exc)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return False
    if run.get("status") != "completed":
        return None
    conclusion = run.get("conclusion")
    if conclusion in _STOPPED_CONCLUSIONS:
        return f"CI {_OVER_BUDGET}: run {run_id} concluded {conclusion}"
    if conclusion != "failure":
        return None
    stopped = [
        str(job.get("name") or "unnamed job")
        for job in run.get("jobs", [])
        if _blocking_leg(str(job.get("name") or ""))
        and job.get("conclusion") in _STOPPED_CONCLUSIONS
    ]
    if stopped:
        return f"CI {_OVER_BUDGET}: {', '.join(stopped)}"
    jobs = [
        job["name"]
        for job in run.get("jobs", [])
        if _blocking_leg(str(job.get("name") or ""))
        and (
            job.get("status") != "completed"
            or job.get("conclusion") not in OK_CONCLUSIONS
        )
    ]
    if not jobs:
        return None
    return {
        "jobs": jobs,
        "urls": [run["url"]] if run.get("url") else [],
        "run_id": run_id,
        "diagnostics": _CI["run_diagnostics"](run, run_id),
        "completed": True,
    }


def marker_path() -> Path:
    """`.release-owner` at THIS worktree's root (round.json is shared by all)."""
    cp = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        encoding="utf-8",
        check=False,
    )
    if cp.returncode:
        _command_failed("git rev-parse --show-toplevel", cp)
    top = cp.stdout.strip()
    return Path(top or Path.cwd()) / ".release-owner"


def read_owner() -> dict[str, Any]:
    try:
        data = json.loads(marker_path().read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def write_owner(branch: str, base: str, cut_at: str) -> None:
    path = marker_path()
    data = {
        "branch": branch,
        "base": base,
        "cut_at": cut_at,
        "worktree": str(path.parent),
    }
    path.write_text(json.dumps(data, indent=1) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")


def clear_owner() -> None:
    marker_path().unlink(missing_ok=True)


def owns(rel: dict[str, Any]) -> bool:
    """This worktree cut the recorded release (marker matches branch + base)."""
    mark = read_owner()
    return bool(
        rel
        and mark.get("branch") == rel.get("branch")
        and mark.get("base") == rel.get("base")
    )


def gate_facts() -> dict[str, Any]:
    cp = rnd("gate", "--json")
    if cp.returncode:
        _command_failed("scripts/round gate --json", cp)
    try:
        data = json.loads(cp.stdout)
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(
            f"scripts/round gate --json unreadable: {_error_detail(exc)}",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def _max_candidate_hours() -> float:
    """Mirror scripts/round's PRECIS_ROUND_MAX_CANDIDATE_HOURS (default 6h).

    Round deploy owns the refusal; this is only the staleness pre-check that
    decides whether a CI re-run is worth triggering first. An unreadable or
    non-positive setting defers to the default, same as the guard.
    """
    try:
        v = float(os.environ.get("PRECIS_ROUND_MAX_CANDIDATE_HOURS", "") or 6.0)
    except ValueError:
        return 6.0
    return v if math.isfinite(v) and v > 0 else 6.0


def _regate_run_id(sha: str) -> str:
    """Newest check.yml run id on `sha`; '' when gh or the run is unreadable."""
    try:
        cp = subprocess.run(
            [
                "gh",
                "run",
                "list",
                "--commit",
                sha,
                "--workflow",
                "check.yml",
                "--limit",
                "1",
                "--json",
                "databaseId",
                "--jq",
                ".[0].databaseId",
            ],
            cwd=ROOT,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            check=False,
            timeout=120,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        print(
            f"gh run list for {sha} unreadable: {_error_detail(exc)}", file=sys.stderr
        )
        return ""
    if cp.returncode:
        print(
            f"gh run list for {sha} failed (exit {cp.returncode}): {cp.stderr.strip()}",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
    return cp.stdout.strip() if cp.returncode == 0 else ""


def _wait_fresh_verdict(head: str, args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """Wait for a re-run verdict on `head` that is green AND inside the age
    limit. 2 on red; 0 on green or on timeout (the deploy then re-checks and
    keeps its own refusal path, same as before this existed)."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + args.ci_timeout * 60
    limit = _max_candidate_hours()
    say(f"waiting for the re-run verdict on {head[:9]}")
    last_stop = ""
    while True:
        facts = gate_facts()
        cand = str(facts.get("candidate") or "")
        age = facts.get("age_hours")
        if (
            cand == head
            and isinstance(age, (int, float))
            and math.isfinite(age)
            and 0 <= age <= limit
        ):
            say(f"verdict fresh on {head[:9]} ({age:.1f}h)")
            return 0
        red = ci_red(head)
        if isinstance(red, str) and red != last_stop:
            say(red)
            last_stop = red
        if isinstance(red, dict):
            print(
                f"re-run CI on {head[:9]} went red — failing: {', '.join(red['jobs'])}",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            for u in red["urls"]:
                print(f"  {u}", file=sys.stderr)
            for line in red.get("diagnostics", []):
                print(f"  {line}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            say(
                f"re-run verdict not green within {args.ci_timeout:g} min — "
                "deploy will apply its own verdict-age refusal if still stale"
            )
            return 0
        time.sleep(args.poll)


def _regate_if_stale(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """When the release head's CI verdict is older than the deploy guard's
    limit, re-run CI on the same sha and wait for a fresh verdict instead of
    letting `round deploy` refuse (the r33 resume that died on age alone).
    0 = proceed to deploy; 2 = the re-run went red."""
    facts = gate_facts()
    cand = str(facts.get("candidate") or "")
    head = str(facts.get("release_head") or "")
    if not cand or not head or cand != head:
        return 0  # not an exact-green release head; deploy's own guards decide
    age = facts.get("age_hours")
    if not isinstance(age, (int, float)) or not math.isfinite(age) or age < 0:
        return 0  # unknown age keeps the existing refusal path
    limit = _max_candidate_hours()
    if age <= limit:
        return 0
    say(
        f"verdict for {cand[:9]} is {age:.1f}h old (limit {limit:g}h) — "
        "re-running CI on the same sha instead of refusing"
    )
    run_id = _regate_run_id(cand)
    if not run_id:
        say(
            "no check.yml run found on that sha to re-run — deploy proceeds to its own verdict check"
        )
        return 0
    try:
        cp = subprocess.run(
            ["gh", "run", "rerun", run_id],
            cwd=ROOT,
            check=False,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            timeout=120,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        say(f"gh run rerun {run_id} failed: {_error_detail(exc)}")
        return 0
    if cp.returncode != 0:
        say(
            f"gh run rerun {run_id} failed ({cp.stderr.strip() or cp.returncode}) — deploy proceeds to its own verdict check"
        )
        return 0
    say(f"re-ran check.yml run {run_id} on {cand[:9]}")
    return _wait_fresh_verdict(head, args)


#: ``release_cut`` exports the pass todo id here when it launches the cut
#: (gr675506, Reto 2026-11-14: exactly one cutter, the system).
RELEASE_PASS_ENV = "PRECIS_RELEASE_PASS"


def release_pass() -> str:
    """The factory release pass marker, else ``""``."""
    return os.environ.get(RELEASE_PASS_ENV, "").strip()


def hand_cut_warning() -> str | None:
    """A hand cut's warning, or None under the factory release pass. A
    later release turns this into a refusal (gr675506)."""
    if release_pass():
        return None
    return (
        f"warning: {RELEASE_PASS_ENV} is unset — releases are cut only by the"
        " factory release pass (Reto's ruling 2026-11-14, gr675506); this hand"
        " cut will be refused in a later release"
    )


def next_line(sha: str) -> str:
    return (
        f"next: verify runtime, then scripts/round deploy --confirm-runtime {sha} "
        "--runtime-evidence '<observed services/boot SHAs/readiness/session MCP>' "
        "&& scripts/round close"
    )


# ── coupled-commit guard (gr687874) ────────────────────────────────
#
# Reto's ruling 2026-11-15: "commit A requires B" is a release-cutting
# restriction — a release must carry both parts or neither. The pairing
# is declared at land time as commit-message trailers; ship preserves a
# branch's trailer block verbatim through the squash (gr517803), so a
# trailer written on the slice's commits reaches main unchanged:
#
#   Change-Set: <id> [<n>]   every commit on main's first-parent line
#                            carrying the same <id> is one set of <n>
#                            members (n defaults to 2 — write the count
#                            for a bigger set; <id> is one token). A cut
#                            keeps landed members together. The wait for
#                            missing siblings can age out.
#   Requires: <sha>          the commit requires commit <sha> (7-40 hex,
#                            a commit reachable from origin/main); a cut
#                            carries both or neither. A <sha> already on
#                            origin/prod satisfies the pairing.
#
# _split_set_cut applies the rule to every cut target S over the
# first-parent commits in (origin/prod..origin/main]:
#
#  - A landed pairing the cut would SPLIT (members on both sides of S)
#    HOLDS — a pinned cut must not ship half. release_cut classes the
#    `holding: ` line a wait (attempt-free), the retarget marker moves
#    the next cut to origin/main's tip, and a tip cut never splits a
#    landed set. Requires is checked in both directions: a pinned cut at
#    B with A above it, A requiring B, holds.
#  - An incomplete Change-Set (fewer than n members, counting the ones
#    origin/prod already holds as shipped) moves the cut BELOW its oldest
#    landed member — the cut ships none of the set. From a tip cut, or
#    when the move would leave nothing shippable, it HOLDS instead (a
#    pipelined cut can take only the tip anyway). After 24 hours from the
#    oldest unshipped member's committer time, the missing members stop
#    blocking the cut. Use committer time so an old author date on a
#    newly landed squash commit does not shorten the wait.
#  - A Requires value that is not a commit reachable from origin/main (a
#    td/gr handle, a pre-squash builder sha, a sibling that never landed)
#    FAILS OPEN: it cannot be honoured, so the cut names it in one
#    warning line on stderr and never holds on it — one bad trailer must
#    not stop every release (td759095 review).
#  - An unreadable history HOLDS — the cut never proceeds unchecked.
#
# Lands stay free; only the cut is constrained, and only where a cut is
# SELECTED (the fresh cut, the red re-cut, a pending cut's recorded base
# that reconciling would still push): resuming or reporting an
# already-recorded release, or finalizing a pending cut whose branch
# origin already holds at its base, is never re-gated on pairings landed
# since (td695907).
#
# The check reads one origin/main snapshot, and that snapshot is what
# authorizes publication: the caller hands it to `round cut --main-at`,
# which refuses with _MAIN_MOVED when its own refresh of main differs
# (a lane landed in between). The fresh cut re-checks on a new fetch and
# retries; the other cut paths hold. Nothing is pushed on that refusal.

#: ``round cut``'s refusal when main moved past ``--main-at`` (scripts/round
#: _MAIN_MOVED) — cut_and_finish maps it to MAIN_MOVED.
_MAIN_MOVED_PHRASE = "origin/main moved since the pairing check"
#: cut_and_finish's result when ``round cut`` refused on _MAIN_MOVED_PHRASE.
MAIN_MOVED = -2

_CHANGE_SET_KEY = "change-set"
_CHANGE_SET_WAIT_SECONDS = 24 * 60 * 60
_REQUIRES_KEY = "requires"
_SHA_TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{7,40}")
_TRAILER_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9-]*):(?:[ \t]+(.*))?$")


def _trailers(body: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """(key, value) pairs of the message's trailer block — the last
    paragraph whose non-blank lines are all `Key: value` (with
    continuation lines folded into the previous value), the same rule
    ship_msg_body applies — so prose mentioning a key is not read."""
    lines = body.splitlines()
    end = len(lines)
    while end > 0 and not lines[end - 1].strip():
        end -= 1
    start = end
    while start > 0 and lines[start - 1].strip():
        start -= 1
    if start < 1 or start >= end:  # no blank line before it: not a block
        return []
    out: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    for ln in lines[start:end]:
        if not ln.strip():
            return []
        m = _TRAILER_LINE_RE.match(ln)
        if m is None:
            if ln[:1] in (" ", "\t") and out:
                value = ln.strip()
                if value:
                    key, prev = out[-1]
                    out[-1] = (key, f"{prev} {value}" if prev else value)
                continue
            return []
        out.append((m.group(1), (m.group(2) or "").strip()))
    return out


def _first_parent_walk(prod: str, tip: str) -> list[tuple[str, int, str]] | None:
    """(sha, committer timestamp, message) of each first-parent commit in (prod..tip],
    NEWEST first (git log's order — the guard's positions index it); None
    when git cannot read the range (the caller holds). With no prod ref
    the walk covers the entire first-parent line through tip."""
    rng = f"{prod}..{tip}" if prod else tip
    out = git(
        "log",
        "--first-parent",
        "--format=%H%x1f%ct%x1f%B%x00",
        rng,
    )
    if out is None:
        return None
    walk: list[tuple[str, int, str]] = []
    for rec in out.split("\0"):
        if not rec.strip():
            continue
        fields = rec.strip("\n").split("\x1f", 2)
        if len(fields) != 3 or not fields[1].isascii() or not fields[1].isdigit():
            return None
        sha, committed_at, body = fields
        walk.append((sha.strip(), int(committed_at), body))
    return walk


def _change_sets(walk: list[tuple[str, int, str]]) -> dict[str, dict[str, Any]]:
    """Change-Set id → {"members": [sha...] (land order), "want": n} —
    the highest declared member count wins."""
    out: dict[str, dict[str, Any]] = {}
    for sha, _committed_at, body in walk:
        for key, value in _trailers(body):
            if key.lower() != _CHANGE_SET_KEY or not value:
                continue
            tokens = value.split()
            cid = tokens[0]
            want = 2
            if len(tokens) > 1 and tokens[1].isdigit() and int(tokens[1]) >= 2:
                want = int(tokens[1])
            ent = out.setdefault(cid, {"members": [], "want": 2})
            ent["want"] = max(ent["want"], want)
            if sha not in ent["members"]:
                ent["members"].insert(0, sha)  # land order, oldest first
    return out


def _requires(walk: list[tuple[str, int, str]]) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """(commit sha, raw value) of every Requires trailer (walk order;
    evaluation order is irrelevant)."""
    return [
        (sha, value)
        for sha, _committed_at, body in walk
        for key, value in _trailers(body)
        if key.lower() == _REQUIRES_KEY
    ]


def _resolve_requires(raw: str) -> str | None:
    """The full sha a Requires value names, or None when the value is not
    a hex commit id resolvable in this checkout."""
    tokens = raw.split()
    token = tokens[0] if tokens else ""
    if not _SHA_TOKEN_RE.fullmatch(token):
        return None
    return rev(f"{token}^{{commit}}") or None


def _shipped_members(
    prod: str, cids: list[str]
) -> dict[str, tuple[int, int]] | None:
    """Change-Set id → (how many commits origin/prod already holds that
    carry it in their trailer block, the highest member count those
    commits declared), for the ``cids`` asked about; None when git cannot
    read prod's history (the caller holds). No prod ref: nothing has
    shipped."""
    if not prod or not cids:
        return {}
    out = git("log", "-i", "--grep=^change-set:", "--format=%H%x1f%B%x1e", prod)
    if out is None:
        return None
    want = set(cids)
    seen: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    wants: dict[str, int] = {}
    for rec in out.split("\x1e"):
        sha, sep, body = rec.strip("\n").partition("\x1f")
        if not (sep and sha):
            continue
        for key, value in _trailers(body):
            tokens = value.split()
            if key.lower() == _CHANGE_SET_KEY and tokens and tokens[0] in want:
                seen.setdefault(tokens[0], set()).add(sha.strip())
                n = tokens[1] if len(tokens) > 1 else ""
                if n.isdigit() and int(n) >= 2:
                    wants[tokens[0]] = max(wants.get(tokens[0], 2), int(n))
    return {cid: (len(shas), wants.get(cid, 2)) for cid, shas in seen.items()}


def _requires_targets(
    requires: list[tuple[str, str]], pos: dict[str, int], prod: str, tip: str
) -> dict[str, tuple[str, str] | None]:
    """Requires value → (full sha, where): ``line`` on the first-parent
    walk, ``merge`` reachable from origin/main through a merge, ``prod``
    already shipped; None when the value names no commit reachable from
    origin/main — unenforceable, so the guard fails open on it."""
    out: dict[str, tuple[str, str] | None] = {}
    for _a, raw in requires:
        if raw in out:
            continue
        full = _resolve_requires(raw)
        if full is None:
            out[raw] = None
        elif full in pos:
            out[raw] = (full, "line")
        elif prod and ancestor(full, prod):
            out[raw] = (full, "prod")
        elif ancestor(full, tip):
            out[raw] = (full, "merge")
        else:
            out[raw] = None
    return out


def _split_set_cut(s: str, main_at: str = "") -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """(cut sha, hold reason) — the cut sha honoring every declared
    Change-Set/Requires pairing, or the reason to hold when no cut at or
    below S can proceed without splitting one. ``main_at`` is the
    origin/main snapshot to check against (the one the caller passes to
    ``round cut --main-at``); empty reads the current ref. See the
    section header."""
    prod = rev("origin/prod")
    tip = main_at or rev("refs/remotes/origin/main")
    if not tip:
        return s, "origin/main is unreadable — refusing to cut unchecked"
    walk = _first_parent_walk(prod, tip)
    if walk is None:
        return s, "commit history is unreadable — refusing to cut unchecked"
    pos = {sha: i for i, (sha, _committed_at, _body) in enumerate(walk)}  # 0 = newest
    if s not in pos:
        if prod and ancestor(s, prod):
            return s, None  # already shipped; the rollback guard still applies
        return s, f"{s[:9]} is outside the release history"
    sets = _change_sets(walk)
    # Prod may declare a larger set size than main's members do, so every
    # set is looked up, not only the ones main already sees as short.
    shipped = _shipped_members(prod, list(sets))
    if shipped is None:
        return s, "origin/prod's history is unreadable — refusing to cut unchecked"
    requires = _requires(walk)
    targets = _requires_targets(requires, pos, prod, tip)
    cut = pos[s]
    expired_before = time.time() - _CHANGE_SET_WAIT_SECONDS

    def _done(result: tuple[str, str | None]) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
        # One line per unenforceable Requires the cut carries: named, not
        # held on (td759095 review).
        for a, raw in dict.fromkeys(requires):
            if targets[raw] is None and pos[a] >= cut:
                print(
                    f"scripts/release: warning: {a[:9]} Requires {raw[:40]!r}, "
                    "which is not a commit on origin/main — not enforced",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                    flush=True,
                )
        return result

    while True:
        holds: list[str] = []
        drops: list[int] = []  # positions of landed members to cut below
        drop_why: list[str] = []
        expired: list[tuple[str, int]] = []
        for cid, ent in sets.items():
            members = [m for m in ent["members"] if m in pos]
            in_cut = [m for m in members if pos[m] >= cut]
            if not in_cut:
                continue  # entirely above the cut: not this release's problem
            on_prod, prod_want = shipped.get(cid, (0, 2))
            have = len(members) + on_prod  # prod's count as shipped
            want = max(ent["want"], prod_want)  # highest count across both
            if have < want:
                oldest = pos[members[0]]  # members are in land order
                if walk[oldest][1] > expired_before:
                    drops.append(max(pos[m] for m in in_cut))
                    drop_why.append(
                        f"Change-Set {cid} has {have} of {want} members on main"
                    )
                    continue
                expired.append((cid, want - have))
            if any(pos[m] < cut for m in members):
                holds.append(
                    f"Change-Set {cid} is landed but straddles the cut"
                    f" ({len(in_cut)} of {len(members)} members would ship)"
                )
        for a, raw in requires:
            found = targets[raw]
            if found is None or found[1] == "prod":
                continue  # unenforceable (named by _done), or already shipped
            full, where = found
            in_cut_a = pos[a] >= cut
            in_cut_b = (
                pos[full] >= cut if where == "line" else ancestor(full, walk[cut][0])
            )
            if in_cut_a != in_cut_b:
                holds.append(
                    f"{a[:9]} Requires {full[:9]} and the two straddle the cut"
                )
        if holds:
            return _done((s, "; ".join(dict.fromkeys(holds))))
        if not drops:
            for cid, missing in expired:
                sibling = "sibling" if missing == 1 else "siblings"
                print(
                    f"scripts/release: warning: Change-Set {cid}: "
                    f"{missing} {sibling} never landed, no longer pairing",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                    flush=True,
                )
            return _done((walk[cut][0], None))
        why = "; ".join(dict.fromkeys(drop_why))
        if cut == 0 or release_pass():
            # A factory pass records a cut that contains its requested SHA.
            # Pipelined cuts also require the tip.
            return _done((s, f"{why} — waiting for the rest to land"))
        target = max(drops) + 1
        if target >= len(walk):
            return _done((s, f"{why} — cutting below it would leave nothing to ship"))
        cut = target


def _select_cut(s: str, main_at: str = "") -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """The sha to actually cut honouring declared pairings — S itself, the
    retarget below an incomplete set, or the hold reason (gr687874),
    checked against the origin/main snapshot ``main_at`` the cut then pins
    with ``round cut --main-at``. Runs only where a cut is selected; a
    recorded release's resume path checks its own base instead."""
    safe, hold_why = _split_set_cut(s, main_at)
    if hold_why or safe == s:
        return s, hold_why
    say(
        f"change-set guard (gr687874): cutting at {safe[:9]} instead of {s[:9]} "
        "— an incomplete declared set ships none of itself"
    )
    return safe, None


def live_releases(
    state: dict[str, Any],
    shared: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
    branches: list[str] | None = None,
) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
    """Non-terminal records; the shared ``release.round`` row adjudicates a
    forked clone's open records when it is readable (gr582590): an open
    record a higher-numbered row has moved past, or the same release the
    row names terminal or under another branch, is a leftover, not live.
    The row also adds (gr675506): given origin's release ``branches``, a
    non-terminal row naming one of them that no live local record covers
    is live too — a synthetic record tagged ``from_row`` — whatever this
    clone's round.json says. A deploying/deployed record is never disputed: its
    journal is a live rollout a pipelined cut legitimately passes. Row
    terminality runs through the same predicate as the local record, so a
    row's `failed` counts only while its journal shows the rollout never
    launched (gr593418)."""
    rels = [r for r in (state.get("releases") or []) if not _is_terminal(r)]
    if not isinstance(shared, dict):
        return rels
    db_n = shared.get("n")
    db_state = str(shared.get("state") or "")
    db_branch = str(shared.get("branch") or "")
    db_terminal = _is_terminal({"state": db_state, "deploy": shared.get("deploy")})
    out = []
    for r in rels:
        n = r.get("n")
        if (
            r.get("state") in OPEN_STATES
            and type(db_n) is int
            and type(n) is int
            and (
                db_n > n
                or (
                    db_n == n
                    and (db_terminal or (db_branch and db_branch != r.get("branch")))
                )
            )
        ):
            continue
        out.append(r)
    if branches is not None and not db_terminal and db_branch in branches:
        if db_branch not in {str(r.get("branch")) for r in out}:
            out.append(
                {
                    "n": db_n,
                    "branch": db_branch,
                    "base": str(shared.get("base") or ""),
                    "state": db_state,
                    "from_row": True,
                }
            )
    return out


def stale_why(rel: dict[str, Any], prod: str) -> str:
    """The stale-blocker reason for `rel`, or "" when it is not provably
    stale — a deploying/deployed record `round supersede --dry-run`
    accepts because origin/prod already moved past its pin (gr573151). A
    `failed` record whose rollout launched is judged the same way
    (gr724696): prod past its pin makes it a corpse, not a partial rollout
    to resume; the verb refuses a terminal `failed` one."""
    if not (
        rel.get("state") in (*DEPLOYING_STATES, "failed")
        and not rel.get("cut_pending")
        and rel.get("branch")
    ):
        return ""
    try:
        accepted = rnd("supersede", str(rel["branch"]), "--dry-run").returncode == 0
    except OSError:
        accepted = False  # unrunnable supersede ⇒ not provably stale
    if not accepted:
        return ""
    pin = str((rel.get("deployment") or {}).get("sha") or "")
    return (
        f"{rel['branch']} is stale — origin/prod {prod[:9]} moved past"
        f" its pinned {pin[:9]}; clear it with `scripts/round supersede"
        f" r{rel.get('n')}`"
    )


def _dead_open_rel(rels: list[dict[str, Any]], prod: str) -> dict[str, Any] | None:
    """The open record provably dead (gr582590): its branch is absent on
    origin AND origin/prod already contains its base — a forked clone's
    leftover whose payload shipped, not in-flight work. None when nothing
    qualifies or the evidence is incomplete (origin, prod or the base
    unreadable — never retire on a maybe)."""
    if not prod or not rev(prod):
        return None
    rel = next((r for r in rels if r.get("state") in OPEN_STATES), None)
    if rel is None:
        return None
    branch = str(rel.get("branch") or "")
    if not branch or remote(f"refs/heads/{branch}") != "":
        return None
    base = str(rel.get("base") or "")
    if not base or not rev(base) or not ancestor(base, prod):
        return None
    return rel


def _owned_stale(rel: dict[str, Any]) -> bool:
    """The marker claiming ``rel`` is stale when the branch it names is
    gone from origin (gr582590). Open records only: a deploying record's
    journal is its evidence, not the branch, and a pending cut keeps its
    marker — absence is the pending record's whole point. An unreadable
    origin never condemns."""
    if rel.get("cut_pending") or rel.get("state") not in OPEN_STATES:
        return False
    return remote(f"refs/heads/{rel.get('branch')}") == ""


def _release_by_state(state: dict[str, Any], states: tuple[str, ...]) -> dict[str, Any]:
    return next((r for r in live_releases(state) if r.get("state") in states), {})


def _release_deploying(state: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:
    """The deploying-side record a resume still owns: `deploying`/`deployed`,
    or a live `failed` partial rollout — inside `live_releases` a `failed`
    record is necessarily the not-terminal one whose journal shows
    `scripts/deploy` launched (gr593418 round 3)."""
    return _release_by_state(state, (*DEPLOYING_STATES, "failed"))


def release_label(state: dict[str, Any]) -> str:
    """``r<N>`` from the recorded release branch, else the round number."""
    rel = _release_deploying(state) or _release_by_state(state, OPEN_STATES)
    branch = str(rel.get("branch") or "")
    return branch.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] if branch else f"r{state.get('n')}"


#: Longest the deploy path waits on scripts/gripe-note (its own prod call
#: is capped at 25 s); past this the note is abandoned, the deploy goes on.
NOTE_TIMEOUT_S = 30.0


def note_gripes(prev: str, head: str, text: str) -> None:
    """Comment each gripe a commit in prev..head fixes (scripts/gripe-note).

    Best-effort: the rollout already happened, so nothing here fails or
    holds the deploy past :data:`NOTE_TIMEOUT_S`.
    """
    if not head or prev == head:
        return
    rng = f"{prev}..{head}" if prev else head
    # Own process group: gripe-note runs under `uv run`, whose python child
    # would outlive a kill of the direct child and keep the pipe open.
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(
            [str(HERE / "gripe-note"), "--rev", rng, "--text", text],
            cwd=str(ROOT),
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            start_new_session=True,
        )
    except OSError as e:
        say(f"gripe-note: {_error_detail(e)} — no deploy comments posted")
        return
    try:
        out, err = proc.communicate(timeout=NOTE_TIMEOUT_S)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        _, err = proc.communicate()
        say(
            f"gripe-note: TimeoutExpired after {NOTE_TIMEOUT_S:g}s — no deploy comments"
        )
        _command_failed(
            "scripts/gripe-note", subprocess.CompletedProcess(proc.args, 124, "", err)
        )
        return
    if proc.returncode:
        _command_failed(
            "scripts/gripe-note",
            subprocess.CompletedProcess(proc.args, proc.returncode, out, err),
        )
    if out.strip():
        say(out.strip())


#: The prod record of the deployed head: an ``app_settings`` row, registered
#: in ``precis.settings.REGISTRY`` (this script cannot import precis; a test
#: pins the two literals equal). ``precis.plans.gates`` reads it for the
#: ``sha-in-prod`` / ``tests-green`` gates — the server has no git.
PROD_HEAD_KEY = "release.prod_head"
#: Abbreviation length of the recorded ``landed`` shas (gates match by prefix).
LANDED_SHA_LEN = 12
#: Most shas kept in ``landed``; the oldest fall off first. 2000 x 12 chars is
#: about 26 KB, comfortably inside an argv slot.
MAX_LANDED = 2000
#: Cap on each of the two prod calls (read the old record, write the new).
RECORD_TIMEOUT_S = 40.0

#: Runs ``scripts/prod-precis <args>``; returns (returncode, stdout, stderr).
#: Tests pass a fake; a real call raises OSError/TimeoutExpired.
Transport = Callable[[list[str]], tuple[int, str, str]]


def _prod_precis(
    args: list[str], timeout: float = RECORD_TIMEOUT_S
) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    """The real transport: scripts/prod-precis from a tree that may write.

    A deploy moves origin/prod past this checkout, and prod-precis refuses a
    write from a tree behind it, so reuse scripts/gripe-note's tree choice
    (this one, else the prod clone) and its process-group-safe timeout.
    """
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util

    path = HERE / "gripe-note"
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("gripe_note", str(path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("gripe_note", loader)
    if spec is None:
        raise OSError("cannot load scripts/gripe-note")
    gn = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    exec(compile(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"), str(path), "exec"), gn.__dict__)
    cli = os.environ.get("PRECIS_GRIPE_NOTE_CLI", "").strip()
    base = (
        shlex.split(cli)
        if cli
        else [str(gn.write_tree(HERE.parent) / "scripts" / "prod-precis")]
    )
    cp = gn.run_capped([*base, *args], timeout)
    return cp.returncode, cp.stdout, cp.stderr


def _record_skip_reason() -> str | None:
    """Why the real transport must not run, or None. Same seams as
    scripts/gripe-note: PRECIS_GRIPE_NOTE=0, no prod DSN on this host
    (PRECIS_GRIPE_NOTE_CLI replaces the check). A PRECIS_ROUND_ROOT override
    means a test rig or a foreign checkout, never the repo that deploys."""
    if os.environ.get("PRECIS_GRIPE_NOTE", "1") == "0":
        return "PRECIS_GRIPE_NOTE=0"
    if HERE.parent != ROOT:
        return "PRECIS_ROUND_ROOT names another checkout"
    dsn = Path.home() / ".secrets" / "pw" / "PRECIS_DATABASE_URL"
    if not os.environ.get("PRECIS_GRIPE_NOTE_CLI", "").strip() and not dsn.is_file():
        return "no prod DSN on this host"
    return None


def landed_shas(prev: str, head: str) -> list[str]:
    """Abbreviated shas in ``prev..head`` (``head`` alone with no ``prev``),
    newest first; empty when git cannot read the range."""
    rng = f"{prev}..{head}" if prev else head
    out = git("rev-list", "--abbrev-commit", f"--abbrev={LANDED_SHA_LEN}", rng)
    return [s[:LANDED_SHA_LEN] for s in (out or "").split()]


_GET_RE = re.compile(
    rf"^{re.escape(PROD_HEAD_KEY)} = (.*) \(layer=(\w+)\)$", re.MULTILINE
)


def parse_prod_head(stdout: str) -> dict[str, Any] | None:
    """The record from ``precis settings get`` output, or None (unset,
    unparsable, not the DB layer)."""
    m = _GET_RE.search(stdout)
    if m is None or m.group(2) != "db":
        return None
    try:
        rec = json.loads(m.group(1))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return rec if isinstance(rec, dict) else None


def prod_head_record(
    head: str,
    landed: list[str],
    previous: dict[str, Any] | None,
    now: datetime,
    *,
    release: str | None = None,
) -> dict[str, Any]:
    """``{head, deployed_at (UTC Z), landed[, release]}``; ``landed`` is
    this deploy's shas, then the previous record's head and landed (so a
    sha that rode an earlier release still reads as in prod), deduped and
    capped. ``release`` is the ``r<N>`` label the deploy ran under —
    gripe_validate names it in its close comments. A rollback keeps the
    rolled-back-over shas; only a new deploy ever trims."""
    merged: list[str] = []
    seen: set[str] = set()
    old = [str(previous["head"])[:LANDED_SHA_LEN]] if previous else []
    old += [str(s) for s in (previous or {}).get("landed") or []]
    for s in [*landed, *old]:
        key = s[:LANDED_SHA_LEN]
        if key and key not in seen:
            seen.add(key)
            merged.append(key)
    rec = {
        "head": head,
        "deployed_at": now.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
        "landed": merged[:MAX_LANDED],
    }
    if release:
        rec["release"] = release
    return rec


def _record_base(prev: str, previous: dict[str, Any] | None) -> str:
    """Where this deploy's landed range starts: the old record's head when
    git has that commit, else ``prev``. ``rev-list base..head`` then names
    every commit no record covered yet, whatever moved origin/prod since."""
    old = str((previous or {}).get("head") or "")
    if old and git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{old}^{{commit}}"):
        return old
    return prev


def record_prod_head(
    prev: str,
    head: str,
    *,
    transport: Transport | None = None,
    now: datetime | None = None,
    landed: list[str] | None = None,
    release: str | None = None,
) -> str:
    """Write the deployed-head record into prod; returns one status line.

    ``prev`` is origin/prod before the deploy. Reads the old record through
    the same ``scripts/prod-precis settings get`` call path, merges, and
    writes with ``settings set``. Best-effort like :func:`note_gripes`: the
    rollout already happened, so any fault is reported, never raised.

    The landed range starts at the old RECORD's head, not at ``prev``, when
    git knows that commit (:func:`_record_base`): a deploy that moved
    origin/prod without writing the record (a raw ``scripts/deploy``) left
    its shas out of ``landed`` for good, and every sha-in-prod gate on them
    read "not in prod yet" until the rescue tick filed one ask each
    (gr666159: 180 deployed shas missing, 107 asks).
    """
    if not head:
        return "prod head not recorded: no head sha"
    if transport is None and (why := _record_skip_reason()):
        return f"prod head not recorded: {why}"
    send = transport or _prod_precis
    when = now or datetime.now(UTC)
    try:
        rc, out, _err = send(["settings", "get", PROD_HEAD_KEY])
        if rc and _err.strip():
            _command_failed(
                f"scripts/prod-precis settings get {PROD_HEAD_KEY}",
                subprocess.CompletedProcess([], rc, out, _err),
            )
        previous = parse_prod_head(out) if rc == 0 else None
        if landed is not None:
            shas = landed
        else:
            shas = landed_shas(_record_base(prev, previous), head)
        rec = prod_head_record(head, shas, previous, when, release=release)
        value = json.dumps(rec, separators=(",", ":"))
        rc, _out, err = send(["settings", "set", PROD_HEAD_KEY, value])
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        return f"prod head not recorded: {_error_detail(e)}"
    if rc != 0:
        detail = re.sub(r"[a-z]+://\S+", "<url>", err.strip())
        return f"prod head not recorded (exit {rc}): {detail}"
    return f"prod head recorded: {head[:9]} (+{len(shas)} landed)"


# ── the shared release.round row ─────────────────────────────────
#
#: ``app_settings`` key ``scripts/round`` publishes the active release's
#: record to (same literal in ``scripts/round.ROUND_KEY`` and
#: ``precis.plans.release.ROUND_KEY`` — tests pin them). Read-only here:
#: the row adjudicates a clone-local record that may have forked
#: (gr582590), so a stale ``round.json`` is not the only say on which
#: release is live.
ROUND_KEY = "release.round"
#: Cap on the shared-record read — a status read must never stall a release.
STATUS_TIMEOUT_S = 3.0

_ROUND_ROW_RE = re.compile(
    rf"^{re.escape(ROUND_KEY)} = (.*) \(layer=(\w+)\)$", re.MULTILINE
)


def read_round_record() -> dict[str, Any] | None:
    """The shared ``release.round`` row (``scripts/round._read_round_record``),
    or None on any failure — a read problem prints nothing and the local
    record stands alone."""
    if _record_skip_reason():
        return None
    try:
        rc, out, _err = _prod_precis(
            ["settings", "get", ROUND_KEY], timeout=STATUS_TIMEOUT_S
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    m = _ROUND_ROW_RE.search(out)
    if m is None or m.group(2) != "db":
        return None
    try:
        row = json.loads(m.group(1))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return row if isinstance(row, dict) else None


#: The prod record of CI verdicts on release candidates (``app_settings``,
#: registered in ``precis.settings.REGISTRY``; a test pins the literals
#: equal). ``precis.plans.gates`` reads it first for ``tests-green`` — the
#: verdict is known here before the deploy, which is the whole point.
CI_VERDICTS_KEY = "release.ci_verdicts"
#: Most verdict rows kept; the oldest fall off first. Rows hold abbreviated
#: shas (~130 bytes each), so 500 stay near 65 KB, inside one 128 KB argv slot.
MAX_CI_VERDICTS = 500

_VERDICTS_GET_RE = re.compile(
    rf"^{re.escape(CI_VERDICTS_KEY)} = (.*) \(layer=(\w+)\)$", re.MULTILINE
)


def parse_ci_verdicts(stdout: str) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
    """The rows from ``precis settings get`` output; ``[]`` when unset,
    unparsable or not the DB layer."""
    m = _VERDICTS_GET_RE.search(stdout)
    if m is None or m.group(2) != "db":
        return []
    try:
        rows = json.loads(m.group(1))
    except ValueError:
        return []
    return [r for r in rows if isinstance(r, dict)] if isinstance(rows, list) else []


def ci_verdict_rows(
    sha: str,
    rng: str,
    verdict: str,
    run_id: str,
    previous: list[dict[str, Any]],
    now: datetime,
) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
    """``previous`` plus ``{sha, range, verdict, run_id, at (UTC Z)}``,
    deduped by sha keeping the newest, capped at :data:`MAX_CI_VERDICTS`.
    Shas are abbreviated to :data:`LANDED_SHA_LEN` (gates match by prefix).
    A red never replaces a ``waived`` row on the same sha — the waiver is
    a standing decision only a fresh green supersedes."""
    row = {
        "sha": sha[:LANDED_SHA_LEN],
        "range": rng,
        "verdict": verdict,
        "run_id": str(run_id or ""),
        "at": now.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
    }
    if verdict == "red" and any(
        isinstance(r.get("waived"), dict)
        and str(r["waived"].get("run_id") or "") == row["run_id"] != ""
        for r in previous
        if str(r.get("sha", ""))[:LANDED_SHA_LEN] == row["sha"]
    ):
        # The red-test waiver (precis.plans.red_waiver) is a standing
        # decision on ONE run: a red read from that same run (a gate that
        # could not read the waiver, a retried poll) must not erase the
        # row. A red from a different run on the head is that run's own
        # verdict and replaces it, as does any green.
        return list(previous)
    kept = [r for r in previous if str(r.get("sha", ""))[:LANDED_SHA_LEN] != row["sha"]]
    return [*kept, row][-MAX_CI_VERDICTS:]


def record_ci_verdict(
    sha: str,
    rng: str,
    verdict: str,
    run_id: str = "",
    *,
    transport: Transport | None = None,
    now: datetime | None = None,
) -> str:
    """Append a CI verdict for candidate ``sha`` to prod; returns one status
    line. ``rng`` is ``<prev_prod>..<sha>``; ``verdict`` is ``green`` or
    ``red``. Same path and skip reasons as :func:`record_prod_head`, and the
    same best-effort contract: a fault is reported, never raised.
    """
    if not sha:
        return "ci verdict not recorded: no sha"
    if verdict not in ("green", "red"):
        return f"ci verdict not recorded: verdict {verdict!r} is not green|red"
    if transport is None and (why := _record_skip_reason()):
        return f"ci verdict not recorded: {why}"
    send = transport or _prod_precis
    when = now or datetime.now(UTC)
    try:
        rc, out, _err = send(["settings", "get", CI_VERDICTS_KEY])
        if rc and _err.strip():
            _command_failed(
                f"scripts/prod-precis settings get {CI_VERDICTS_KEY}",
                subprocess.CompletedProcess([], rc, out, _err),
            )
        previous = parse_ci_verdicts(out) if rc == 0 else []
        rows = ci_verdict_rows(sha, rng, verdict, run_id, previous, when)
        value = json.dumps(rows, separators=(",", ":"))
        rc, _out, err = send(["settings", "set", CI_VERDICTS_KEY, value])
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        return f"ci verdict not recorded: {_error_detail(e)}"
    if rc != 0:
        detail = re.sub(r"[a-z]+://\S+", "<url>", err.strip())
        return f"ci verdict not recorded (exit {rc}): {detail}"
    return f"ci verdict recorded: {sha[:9]} {verdict}"


def note_ci_verdict(head: str, verdict: str, run_id: str = "") -> None:
    """Record the verdict on release head ``head`` against origin/prod and
    say the status line. Best-effort: never raises."""
    prev = rev("origin/prod")
    rng = f"{prev[:LANDED_SHA_LEN]}..{head[:LANDED_SHA_LEN]}" if prev else head
    try:
        say(record_ci_verdict(head, rng, verdict, run_id))
    except Exception as e:  # the gate step must not die on bookkeeping
        say(f"ci verdict not recorded: {_error_detail(e)}")


#: ``app_settings`` key holding the newest red soak verdict (me580555):
#: ``{sha, run_id, jobs, at}``. A `soak*` leg is non-blocking (the
#: continue-on-error legs keep the run conclusion green), so this row is
#: the debt record — ``scripts/round cut``/``open`` re-read the soak's
#: check-runs on the last gated main sha and refuse on red (the row
#: caches the read), and ``round soak-clear`` amends it with a waiver.
SOAK_RED_KEY = "release.soak_red"


def record_soak_red(
    sha: str,
    run_id: str,
    jobs: list[str],
    *,
    transport: Transport | None = None,
    now: datetime | None = None,
) -> str:
    """Write the ``release.soak_red`` row to prod; returns one status
    line. Same path and skip reasons as :func:`record_ci_verdict`, and the
    same best-effort contract: a fault is reported, never raised. The sha
    is kept whole — `round cut` needs an ancestor test, not a prefix."""
    if not sha:
        return "soak red not recorded: no sha"
    if transport is None and (why := _record_skip_reason()):
        return f"soak red not recorded: {why}"
    send = transport or _prod_precis
    when = now or datetime.now(UTC)
    row = {
        "sha": sha,
        "run_id": str(run_id or ""),
        "jobs": [str(j) for j in jobs],
        "at": when.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
    }
    try:
        rc, _out, err = send(
            ["settings", "set", SOAK_RED_KEY, json.dumps(row, separators=(",", ":"))]
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        return f"soak red not recorded: {_error_detail(e)}"
    if rc != 0:
        detail = re.sub(r"[a-z]+://\S+", "<url>", err.strip())
        return f"soak red not recorded (exit {rc}): {detail}"
    return f"soak red recorded: {sha[:9]} run {run_id or '?'}"


def note_soak(head: str) -> None:
    """Record ``release.soak_red`` when the current run's `soak*` legs
    went red, and say the warning line. Best-effort like
    :func:`note_ci_verdict`: never raises, never changes the verdict."""
    try:
        red = soak_red(head)
        if red is False:
            say("soak legs unreadable — no soak verdict recorded")
            return
        if not red:
            return
        say(f"soak red on {head[:9]} (non-blocking): {', '.join(red['jobs'])}")
        for u in red["urls"]:
            say(f"  {u}")
        say(record_soak_red(head, str(red["run_id"]), list(red["jobs"])))
    except Exception as e:  # the gate step must not die on bookkeeping
        say(f"soak verdict not recorded: {_error_detail(e)}")


#: Longest the deploy path waits on scripts/gripe-validate (two prod calls,
#: each capped at 90 s); past this it is abandoned, the release goes on.
VALIDATE_TIMEOUT_S = 200.0


def validate_gripes() -> None:
    """Close eligible verify gripes whose sha is now on origin/prod.

    A live-when:<service> gripe waits for a successful service_config
    lookup showing positive priority anywhere. Validation is best-effort
    after rollout (scripts/gripe-validate, gr541800).
    """
    script = HERE / "gripe-validate"
    if not script.exists() or HERE.parent != ROOT:
        return  # a test rig / foreign checkout (PRECIS_ROUND_ROOT) never writes
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(
            [str(script)],
            cwd=str(ROOT),
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            start_new_session=True,
        )
    except OSError as e:
        say(f"gripe-validate: {_error_detail(e)} — no gripes validated")
        return
    try:
        out, err = proc.communicate(timeout=VALIDATE_TIMEOUT_S)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        _, err = proc.communicate()
        say(f"gripe-validate: TimeoutExpired after {VALIDATE_TIMEOUT_S:g}s")
        _command_failed(
            "scripts/gripe-validate",
            subprocess.CompletedProcess(proc.args, 124, "", err),
        )
        return
    if proc.returncode:
        _command_failed(
            "scripts/gripe-validate",
            subprocess.CompletedProcess(proc.args, proc.returncode, out, err),
        )
    if out.strip():
        say(out.strip())


def pending_sha(rel: dict[str, Any], default: str) -> str:
    dep = rel.get("deployment") or {}
    return str(dep.get("sha") or default)


#: The post-migration probe shells out to scripts/prod-precis (git fetch +
#: uv run + one prod query); cap the whole call well above that.
POST_MIGRATION_CHECK_TIMEOUT_S = 180.0


def warn_post_migration() -> None:
    """Name pending post-migration steps after the deploy's migrate play.

    The assert itself is `precis post-migration-check` — read-only, listing
    NOT VALID constraints and missing per-kind ANN indexes with the command
    that clears each — run against prod through scripts/prod-precis. The
    health-digest ``post_migration_steps`` row reports the same probe
    continuously (gr514955; prod ran weeks on the global-index fallback
    before anyone noticed, gr513663). Best-effort: a missing wrapper or an
    unreadable prod never fails a deploy.
    """
    wrapper = ROOT / "scripts" / "prod-precis"
    if not wrapper.exists():
        say("post-migration check skipped: scripts/prod-precis not in this checkout")
        return
    try:
        cp = subprocess.run(
            [str(wrapper), "post-migration-check"],
            cwd=ROOT,
            check=False,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            timeout=POST_MIGRATION_CHECK_TIMEOUT_S,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        say(f"post-migration check skipped: {_error_detail(e)}")
        return
    if cp.returncode == 0:
        for line in cp.stdout.strip().splitlines():
            say(line)
        return
    tail = (cp.stderr or "").strip().splitlines()
    say(
        f"post-migration check unreadable (rc {cp.returncode}): "
        f"{tail[-1][:200] if tail else 'no output'}"
    )
    _command_failed("scripts/prod-precis post-migration-check", cp)


def warn_hook_drift() -> None:
    """Report drifted live copies of scripts/hooks/* at release time.

    The ~/.claude/hooks globals are hand copies of scripts/hooks/* and the
    user settings' `cmp -s` shims run the GLOBAL whenever it differs — a
    stale global silently serves old code (gr516942). scripts/hook-sync
    only reports (the exact `cp` per drifted file); never fails a release.
    """
    script = ROOT / "scripts" / "hook-sync"
    if not script.exists():
        return
    try:
        cp = subprocess.run(
            ["bash", str(script)],
            cwd=ROOT,
            check=False,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            timeout=30,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        say(f"hook-sync check skipped: {_error_detail(e)}")
        return
    if cp.returncode:
        _command_failed("bash scripts/hook-sync", cp)
    for line in cp.stdout.strip().splitlines():
        say(line)


def wait_ci(branch: str, args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """0 = release head is green; else the exit code to return."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + args.ci_timeout * 60
    warned = False
    last_stop = ""
    say(f"waiting for CI on {branch}")
    while True:
        facts = gate_facts()
        head = str(facts.get("release_head") or "")
        cand = str(facts.get("candidate") or "")
        rbranch = str(facts.get("release_branch") or "")
        if facts.get("candidate_state") == "release_missing" and rbranch == branch:
            return die(1, f"release {branch} is missing on origin — investigate")
        if head and cand == head and rbranch == branch:
            if facts.get("candidate_state") == "waived":
                # The red-test waiver (precis.plans.red_waiver): `round gate`
                # read a green-waived release.ci_verdicts row for this head.
                # Proceed, and do NOT re-record the verdict — that would
                # overwrite the waiver row with a plain green.
                waiver = (facts.get("certificate") or {}).get("waived") or {}
                say(
                    f"CI red on {branch} at {head[:9]} WAIVED by "
                    f"gr{waiver.get('gripe', '?')} — deploying anyway "
                    f"({', '.join(waiver.get('tests') or []) or 'no ids'})"
                )
                return 0  # no soak note: the head's CI was red
            say(f"CI green on {branch} at {head[:9]}")
            note_ci_verdict(head, "green", _regate_run_id(head))
            note_soak(head)
            return 0
        if head and rbranch == branch:
            red = ci_red(head)
            if red is False:
                if not warned:
                    say("gh unreadable: polling `round gate` only (no early red)")
                    warned = True
            elif isinstance(red, dict):
                # `gh run rerun` can lag the old failure: confirm one poll later
                time.sleep(args.poll)
                red = ci_red(head)
            if isinstance(red, str) and red != last_stop:
                say(red)
                last_stop = red
            if isinstance(red, dict):
                note_ci_verdict(head, "red", red["run_id"])
                print(
                    f"CI red on {branch} at {head[:9]} — failing: {', '.join(red['jobs'])}",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                for u in red["urls"]:
                    print(f"  {u}", file=sys.stderr)
                for line in red.get("diagnostics", []):
                    print(f"  {line}", file=sys.stderr)
                if red["run_id"]:
                    print(f"  scripts/ci-failed {red['run_id']}", file=sys.stderr)
                print(
                    f"{branch} left in place: fix via scripts/ship --release, "
                    "or abandon with scripts/round cut --abandon",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                    flush=True,
                )
                return 2
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            return die(
                2,
                f"CI not green after {args.ci_timeout:g} min on {branch} (left in place)",
            )
        time.sleep(args.poll)


def _mark_deploy_failed(branch: str) -> None:
    """Best-effort verdict for a deploy leg that ran and died (gr593418):
    `round fail` moves a `deploying` record to `failed`. A journal whose
    rollout never launched (no `started_epoch`) reads terminal — the next
    cut supersedes it; a launched rollout keeps the record a live partial
    rollout that `deploy` resumes. A `round deploy` refusal before its
    journal write leaves the record open — `fail` refuses and nothing is
    marked."""
    cp = rnd("fail", branch) if branch else rnd("fail")
    if cp.returncode:
        sys.stderr.write(cp.stderr)
    else:
        sys.stdout.write(cp.stdout)
        sys.stdout.flush()


# ── catpath wheel preflight relief (gr584832) ─────────────────────────────
# scripts/deploy's preflight (gr263082) dies unless a pin-matching
# autocatpath wheel is reachable from the controller (a catpath checkout at
# one of the probe paths, or PRECIS_CATPATH_DIR), because the wheelhouse
# seed is the only channel to a plugin host. retospect/catpath is a PRIVATE
# repo: the fix-lane user cannot clone it, so every factory release_deploy
# died in preflight even with the fleet already seeded at the pinned
# version (r243: uv.lock pin 0.50.0, plugin hosts already on 0.50.0). The
# preflight lives in scripts/deploy, which this flow reaches only through
# `round deploy` — so the relief runs here, where the deploy is launched:
# when the lock's pin has NO controller-side wheel source and every
# autocatpath_plugin host's wheelhouse already holds the pin-matching
# wheel, the seed has nothing left to do; hand the deploy the documented
# manual bypass PRECIS_DEPLOY_SKIP_CATPATH_WHEEL=1 instead of dying. Any
# doubt — no pin, a lock below pyproject's floor, a checkout present, an
# unreadable inventory, an unreachable host, a host missing the wheel —
# changes nothing and the preflight decides as before, so a pin MISMATCH
# with no wheel source still fails loudly.
#
# Probing uses the pinned deploy's overlay inventory and the
# same capability map the seed tasks gate on
# (precis_capabilities.autocatpath_plugin; topology lives in the overlay's
# group_vars, so ansible-inventory's merged hostvars answer it directly).


def _probe_run(
    argv: list[str], *, cwd: Path, env: dict[str, str]
) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess[str] | None:
    """One probe command; None when the binary is missing or hangs (the
    relief fails closed and the preflight decides)."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(
            argv,
            cwd=cwd,
            check=False,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            encoding="utf-8",
            env=env,
            timeout=90,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None


def _ansible_probe_env() -> dict[str, str]:
    """PATH with every .venv/bin dropped and PRECIS_ANSIBLE_BIN_DIR first —
    the same choice scripts/deploy's _deploy_sanitize_path makes (a project
    .venv carrying ansible-core would otherwise shadow the full package),
    with VIRTUAL_ENV unset for the same reason."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    parts = [
        p for p in env.get("PATH", "").split(":") if p and not p.endswith("/.venv/bin")
    ]
    bin_dir = env.get("PRECIS_ANSIBLE_BIN_DIR", "").strip()
    if bin_dir:
        parts.insert(0, bin_dir)
    env["PATH"] = ":".join(parts)
    env.pop("VIRTUAL_ENV", None)
    return env


def _autocatpath_lock_pin(lock_text: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """(sha, version) uv.lock pins autocatpath at — the same package-stanza
    read as scripts/lib/autocatpath-wheel.sh's autocatpath_locked_sha /
    autocatpath_locked_version, so this and the preflight never disagree
    about what the lock says."""
    sha = ""
    version = ""
    in_pkg = False
    for line in lock_text.splitlines():
        if line == 'name = "autocatpath"':
            in_pkg = True
            continue
        if not in_pkg:
            continue
        if line.startswith("[["):
            break
        if not sha and line.startswith("source = ") and "#" in line:
            m = re.search(r'#([0-9a-f]{7,40})"', line)
            if m:
                sha = m.group(1)
        elif not version and line.startswith("version = "):
            m = re.match(r'version = "([^"]*)"', line)
            if m:
                version = m.group(1)
    return sha, version


def _catpath_checkout_present(root: Path, home: Path | None = None) -> bool:
    """True when the deploy preflight's probe would find a catpath checkout
    (PRECIS_CATPATH_DIR, else the conventional siblings — the same list
    scripts/lib/autocatpath-wheel.sh's autocatpath_probe_dirs walks). With a
    checkout the preflight does its full provenance job (and may build);
    the relief is only for the no-source case."""
    env_dir = os.environ.get("PRECIS_CATPATH_DIR", "").strip()
    if env_dir:
        return (Path(env_dir) / "pyproject.toml").is_file()
    main_root = root
    cp = _probe_run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"],
        cwd=root,
        env=dict(os.environ),
    )
    if cp is not None and cp.returncode == 0 and cp.stdout.strip():
        main_root = Path(cp.stdout.strip()).parent
    home = home or Path.home()
    for cand in (
        main_root.parent / "catpath",
        home / "work" / "project" / "code" / "catpath",
        home / "catpath",
    ):
        if (cand / "pyproject.toml").is_file():
            return True
    return False


def _overlay_inv_args(root: Path) -> tuple[list[str], Path] | None:
    """Extra Ansible args and working directory for the inventory probe."""
    if os.environ.get("PRECIS_DEPLOY_FROM_TREE", "1") == "":
        cluster = root / (
            os.environ.get("PRECIS_CLUSTER_DIR") or Path.home() / "work" / "cluster"
        )
        return ([], cluster) if cluster.is_dir() else None
    overlay = _deploy_overlay_dir(root)
    if overlay is None:
        return None
    vault = overlay.parent / ".vault-pass"
    if not (overlay.is_dir() and vault.is_file()):
        return None
    return [
        "-i",
        str(overlay / "hosts.yml"),
        "--vault-password-file",
        str(vault),
    ], root / "deploy"


def _plugin_hosts(
    cluster: Path, inv_args: list[str], env: dict[str, str]
) -> dict[str, str] | None:
    """{host: wheelhouse_dir} for every autocatpath_plugin host — the same
    membership test the seed tasks apply
    (`inventory_hostname in precis_capabilities.autocatpath_plugin`), read
    from ansible-inventory's merged hostvars. None when the inventory
    cannot be read (fail closed: no relief)."""
    cp = _probe_run(["ansible-inventory", "--list", *inv_args], cwd=cluster, env=env)
    if cp is None or cp.returncode:
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(cp.stdout)
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return None
    hostvars = data.get("_meta", {}).get("hostvars", {})
    if not isinstance(hostvars, dict):
        return None
    out: dict[str, str] = {}
    for host, vars_ in hostvars.items():
        if not isinstance(vars_, dict):
            continue
        caps = vars_.get("precis_capabilities")
        if not isinstance(caps, dict):
            continue
        if host in (caps.get("autocatpath_plugin") or []):
            out[host] = str(vars_.get("autocatpath_wheelhouse") or "/opt/precis/wheels")
    return out


def _wheelhouse_seeded(
    cluster: Path,
    inv_args: list[str],
    env: dict[str, str],
    hosts: dict[str, str],
    version: str,
) -> bool:
    """True when EVERY plugin host's wheelhouse holds the pin-matching
    wheel — the artifact the seed task would have copied, so its absence
    is exactly what the preflight dies on. One unreachable or missing host
    fails the whole probe (fail closed)."""
    for host, wheelhouse in hosts.items():
        cmd = f"ls -d {shlex.quote(wheelhouse)}/autocatpath-{version}-*.whl 2>/dev/null"
        cp = _probe_run(
            ["ansible", host, "-m", "shell", "-a", cmd, *inv_args],
            cwd=cluster,
            env=env,
        )
        if cp is None or cp.returncode or not cp.stdout.strip():
            return False
    return True


def _version_ge(a: str, b: str) -> bool:
    """version_ge's answer for the plain X.Y.Z release numbers both sides
    are filtered to: numeric segments, a shorter prefix ordering before a
    longer one (sort -V's order, so "0.50" < "0.50.0"). False on anything
    with an empty segment — fail closed, never guess."""
    if (
        not a
        or not b
        or not all(part.isdigit() for v in (a, b) for part in v.split("."))
    ):
        return False
    return tuple(int(p) for p in a.split(".")) >= tuple(int(p) for p in b.split("."))


def _autocatpath_floor(pyproject: Path) -> str | None:
    """The lowest `autocatpath>=X` floor pyproject declares — the same
    read as scripts/lib/autocatpath-wheel.sh's autocatpath_floor (last
    match on a line, then the lowest across lines: a wheel only has to
    clear ONE extra's floor, so the lowest is the gate). None when no
    floor is declared — the deploy then skips the catpath block
    entirely, and there is no preflight to relieve."""
    try:
        lines = pyproject.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    pat = re.compile(r'autocatpath[^"]*>=([0-9][0-9.]*)')
    floors = [ms[-1] for line in lines if (ms := pat.findall(line))]
    if not floors:
        return None
    return min(
        floors,
        key=lambda v: tuple(int(p) for p in v.split(".") if p.isdigit()),
    )


def catpath_preflight_relief() -> str | None:
    """The line to print when this deploy should run with
    PRECIS_DEPLOY_SKIP_CATPATH_WHEEL=1, else None. Read the whole story at
    the section header above (gr584832)."""
    if os.environ.get("PRECIS_DEPLOY_SKIP_CATPATH_WHEEL"):
        return None
    if "autocatpath_wheel" in os.environ.get("PRECIS_DEPLOY_EXTRA_VARS", ""):
        return None
    lock = ROOT / "uv.lock"
    if not lock.is_file():
        return None
    try:
        sha, version = _autocatpath_lock_pin(lock.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except OSError:
        return None
    # The version is interpolated into a remote glob — only a plain release
    # number may reach it.
    if not version or not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9][0-9.]*", version):
        return None
    # The gr499104 guard the bypass would otherwise skip: the locked
    # version must still clear pyproject's floor. Below it the wheelhouse
    # holds a wheel no extra can resolve and the on-host install fails
    # mid-run — exactly what the preflight's die exists to prevent, so it
    # keeps it. No declared floor means the deploy skips the catpath block
    # outright and needs no relief.
    floor = _autocatpath_floor(ROOT / "pyproject.toml")
    if floor is None or not _version_ge(version, floor):
        return None
    if _catpath_checkout_present(ROOT):
        return None
    resolved = _overlay_inv_args(ROOT)
    if resolved is None:
        return None
    inv_args, cluster = resolved
    env = _ansible_probe_env()
    hosts = _plugin_hosts(cluster, inv_args, env)
    pin = f"{sha[:12]} (v{version})" if sha else f"v{version}"
    if hosts is None:
        say(
            "catpath preflight relief (gr584832): the inventory could not be "
            "read — leaving the catpath wheel preflight to decide"
        )
        return None
    if not hosts:
        return (
            "catpath preflight relief (gr584832): no autocatpath_plugin hosts "
            f"in the inventory — nothing to seed for uv.lock pin {pin}; deploy "
            "runs with PRECIS_DEPLOY_SKIP_CATPATH_WHEEL=1"
        )
    if not _wheelhouse_seeded(cluster, inv_args, env, hosts, version):
        return None
    return (
        "catpath preflight relief (gr584832): every autocatpath_plugin host "
        f"({', '.join(sorted(hosts))}) already holds autocatpath-{version} in "
        f"its wheelhouse — the seed uv.lock pin {pin} needs is done; deploy "
        "runs with PRECIS_DEPLOY_SKIP_CATPATH_WHEEL=1"
    )


def _deploy_overlay_dir(root: Path) -> Path | None:
    """Overlay for the detached SHA render in scripts/deploy.

    The detached tree has no private inventory. Keep this resolution in
    step with release_cut.overlay_refusal and scripts/deploy.
    """
    if override := os.environ.get("PRECIS_OVERLAY_DIR"):
        return root / override
    cp = _probe_run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"],
        cwd=root,
        env=dict(os.environ),
    )
    common = cp.stdout.strip() if cp is not None and cp.returncode == 0 else ""
    return Path(common).parent / "deploy" / "inventory" if common else None


def _factory_overlay_refusal(root: Path) -> str | None:
    """Why a factory deploy must not run, else ``None`` (gr700433).

    ``release_deploy`` launches ``scripts/release --deploy-only`` with
    ``PRECIS_RELEASE_PASS`` set; under that marker the resolved overlay
    must be its own git repo carrying the deploy-helper remote — the two
    things ``overlay_sync_preflight`` needs before it can reconcile. The
    controller's 20k play provisions that clone and 20b copies its sibling
    vault pass. The gate catches legacy file copies, which overlay-sync
    otherwise skips (gr700433). The legacy
    escape (``PRECIS_DEPLOY_FROM_TREE=``) and the drift override
    (``PRECIS_DEPLOY_ALLOW_OVERLAY_DRIFT``) opt out as they do in
    ``scripts/deploy``; a hand run without the marker keeps the library's
    documented plain-directory default."""
    if not release_pass():
        return None
    if os.environ.get("PRECIS_DEPLOY_FROM_TREE", "1") == "":
        return None
    overlay = _deploy_overlay_dir(root)
    if overlay is None:
        return (
            "scripts/release: the factory deploy refused — cannot resolve "
            "the main checkout for the deploy overlay — no host was touched."
        )
    vault = overlay.parent / ".vault-pass"
    if not (overlay.is_dir() and vault.is_file()):
        return (
            f"scripts/release: the factory deploy refused — no deploy overlay "
            f"(needs the inventory dir {overlay} and {vault}). "
            "See docs/runbooks/cluster-overlay-sync.md — no host was touched."
        )
    remote = os.environ.get("PRECIS_OVERLAY_REMOTE") or "deploy-helper"
    env = dict(os.environ)
    cp = _probe_run(
        ["git", "-C", str(overlay), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
        cwd=root,
        env=env,
    )
    top = cp.stdout.strip() if cp is not None and cp.returncode == 0 else ""
    why = ""
    if not top or os.path.realpath(top) != os.path.realpath(overlay):
        why = f"{overlay} is a plain directory, not the overlay's own git clone"
    else:
        cp = _probe_run(
            ["git", "-C", str(overlay), "remote", "get-url", remote],
            cwd=root,
            env=env,
        )
        if cp is None or cp.returncode != 0:
            why = f"{overlay} has no '{remote}' remote"
    if not why:
        return None
    if os.environ.get("PRECIS_DEPLOY_ALLOW_OVERLAY_DRIFT") or os.environ.get(
        "PRECIS_DEPLOY_ALLOW_STALE_OVERLAY"
    ):
        say(
            f"overlay gate: WARNING: {why} — overlay-sync cannot reconcile "
            "it; PRECIS_DEPLOY_ALLOW_OVERLAY_DRIFT is set — rendering this "
            "copy as-is"
        )
        return None
    return (
        f"scripts/release: the factory deploy refused — {why}, so "
        "overlay-sync's preflight would skip it and the deploy would render "
        "a stale inventory and vault (gr700433). Make it a clone of the "
        f"overlay with the '{remote}' remote "
        "(docs/runbooks/cluster-overlay-sync.md), or set "
        "PRECIS_DEPLOY_ALLOW_OVERLAY_DRIFT=1 to render this copy as-is — "
        "no host was touched."
    )


def deploy_and_close(
    args: argparse.Namespace, sha: str, *, finish_pointer: bool = False
) -> int:
    if not finish_pointer and not getattr(args, "dry_run", False):
        refusal = _factory_overlay_refusal(ROOT)
        if refusal is not None:
            return die(DEPLOY_RC_UNTOUCHED, refusal)
    stale_rc = 0 if finish_pointer else _regate_if_stale(args)
    if stale_rc:
        return stale_rc
    before = round_state() or {}
    finished = _release_deploying(before) or _release_by_state(before, OPEN_STATES)
    label = release_label(before)
    prev = rev("origin/prod")
    command = ("deploy", "--finish-pointer") if finish_pointer else ("deploy",)
    deploy_env = None
    relief = catpath_preflight_relief()
    if relief:
        say(relief)
        deploy_env = {**os.environ, "PRECIS_DEPLOY_SKIP_CATPATH_WHEEL": "1"}
    rc = rnd(*command, stream=True, env=deploy_env).returncode
    if rc in (0, 3):
        # The rollout ran, so its migrate play did too — name whatever
        # post-migration steps it left pending (gr514955). Best-effort.
        warn_post_migration()
        # Tell prod what is deployed (the server has no git): the
        # sha-in-prod / tests-green gates read this. Best-effort.
        cur = round_state() or {}
        head = pending_sha(_release_deploying(cur), sha)
        say(record_prod_head(prev, head, release=label))
        if getattr(args, "deploy_only", ""):
            # The release_deploy op parses this to record the prod head itself.
            say(f"RELEASE_DEPLOYED: {label} {head} {prev or '-'}")
    if rc == 3:
        # The release owner's path: rollout done, runtime confirmed later by
        # `scripts/round deploy --confirm-runtime`. The deploy note posts
        # here, the one exit of this script a deploy takes in practice.
        note_gripes(
            prev, head, f"deployed {label} ({{sha}}); runtime confirmation pending"
        )
        validate_gripes()
        say(next_line(head))
        return 3
    if rc != 0:
        if finish_pointer:
            return die(
                rc,
                "release finish refused — see the reason above; next: resolve it "
                "and re-run scripts/round deploy --finish-pointer",
            )
        if rc == DEPLOY_RC_UNTOUCHED:
            # gr730609: scripts/deploy refused before any rollout step and
            # `round deploy` put the record back — there is no failed leg to
            # mark, and the release must stay live for the re-run.
            return die(
                rc,
                "deploy refused before any rollout — no host was touched and "
                "the release record is unchanged; resolve the reason above and "
                "re-run scripts/release (it resumes) or scripts/round deploy",
            )
        if getattr(args, "deploy_only", ""):
            # gr593418: the deploy leg ran and died — `failed` marks it:
            # terminal while its journal shows `scripts/deploy` never
            # launched, so the next cut supersedes the corpse. A refusal
            # before any work (the record never journaled) leaves it open,
            # and a failed record whose rollout launched stays a live
            # partial rollout — both keep the resume message below.
            _mark_deploy_failed(str(finished.get("branch") or ""))
            after = round_state() or {}
            branch = str(finished.get("branch") or "")
            if branch and not any(
                r.get("branch") == branch for r in live_releases(after)
            ):
                return die(
                    rc,
                    "deploy failed — the next `scripts/round cut` supersedes"
                    " the record",
                )
        return die(
            rc,
            "deploy failed — the release pin is retained; fix and re-run "
            "scripts/release (it resumes) or scripts/round deploy",
        )
    validate_gripes()
    cur = round_state() or {}
    if cur.get("open") and cur.get("deployed") and not live_releases(cur):
        if rnd("close", stream=True).returncode:
            return 0  # deployed; keep the marker, a re-run retries the close
    if owns(finished):
        clear_owner()
    return 0


def run(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    warn_hook_drift()  # gr516942: report (never write) ~/.claude hook drift
    if getattr(args, "record_prod_head", ""):
        # scripts/deploy's hook: a raw deploy moves origin/prod, so it owes
        # prod the record too (gr666159). Best-effort; always exit 0.
        head = rev(args.record_prod_head)
        if getattr(args, "dry_run", False):
            say(f"dry-run: would record prod head {(head or '?')[:9]}")
        else:
            say(record_prod_head("", head or "", release=None))
        return 0
    if args.deploy_only:
        return deploy_only(args)
    races = 0
    while True:
        fetch()
        if args.sha:
            s = rev(args.sha)
            if not s:
                return die(1, f"scripts/release: {args.sha} is not a commit here")
        else:
            s = rev("origin/main")
            if not s:
                return die(1, "scripts/release: cannot resolve origin/main")
        s9 = s[:9]
        line = (
            git("rev-list", "--first-parent", "refs/remotes/origin/main") or ""
        ).split()
        if s not in line:
            return die(
                1, f"scripts/release: {s9} is not on origin/main's first-parent line"
            )
        cur = round_state()
        if cur is None:
            return die(1, "scripts/release: cannot read `scripts/round status --json`")
        prod = remote("refs/heads/prod") or ""
        if prod and not rev(prod):
            git("fetch", "-q", "origin", "prod")
        dead = _dead_open_rel(live_releases(cur), prod)
        if dead is not None:
            # gr582590: a [cut] record whose branch origin lost and whose
            # base prod holds is a forked clone's leftover, not in-flight
            # work — retire it rather than block on it forever. The probe
            # reads the local records BEFORE the shared row's filter: a
            # row that supersedes the record hides it from `live` but not
            # from `round cut`, which still refuses on its cut_pending.
            if args.dry_run:
                say(
                    f"would retire stale {dead['branch']} (gone from origin, "
                    f"base {str(dead.get('base'))[:9]} on prod)"
                )
            else:
                say(
                    f"{dead['branch']} is gone from origin and prod holds its "
                    f"base {str(dead.get('base'))[:9]} — retiring the stale record"
                )
                cp = rnd("cut", "--abandon")
                sys.stdout.write(cp.stdout)
                if cp.returncode:
                    sys.stderr.write(cp.stderr)
                cur = round_state() or cur
        shared = read_round_record()
        live = live_releases(cur, shared)
        open_rel = next((r for r in live if r.get("state") in OPEN_STATES), None)
        if (
            open_rel is not None
            and not open_rel.get("cut_pending")
            and remote(f"refs/heads/{open_rel['branch']}") == ""
        ):
            # gr593418: the open record's branch is gone from origin — the
            # pass was abandoned elsewhere and the record is dead, not in
            # flight. `round cut` supersedes it below (case (a)). A lookup
            # FAILURE (None) keeps it live, and `cut_pending` keeps its
            # own reconcile — neither is this rule's business.
            live = [r for r in live if r is not open_rel]
            open_rel = None
        dep_rel = _release_deploying(cur) or None
        owned = next((r for r in live if owns(r)), None)
        if owned is not None and _owned_stale(owned):
            # A marker is evidence only while the branch exists on origin
            # (gr582590): a dead branch's marker is discarded, not resumed.
            say(f".release-owner for {owned['branch']} is stale — discarded")
            clear_owner()
            owned = None
        rel = owned or open_rel or dep_rel or {}
        mine = owned is not None
        if args.adopt and rel and not args.dry_run and not mine:
            print(
                f"warning: adopting release {rel.get('branch')} cut elsewhere — "
                "the cutting session must not also deploy it",
                file=sys.stderr,
                flush=True,
            )
            write_owner(
                str(rel.get("branch")), str(rel.get("base")), str(rel.get("cut_at"))
            )
            mine = True
        owner = read_owner()
        deployed = cur.get("deployed") or {}
        closing = bool(
            cur.get("open")
            and deployed
            and not live
            and owner.get("branch")
            and owner.get("branch") == deployed.get("branch")
        )
        if closing and not args.dry_run:
            # we deployed this round and its close never landed: retry it
            cp = rnd("close")
            sys.stdout.write(cp.stdout)
            if cp.returncode:
                sys.stderr.write(cp.stderr)
                print("warning: `round close` failed; re-run retries", file=sys.stderr)
            else:
                clear_owner()
                cur = round_state() or cur
        elif owner and not mine and not args.dry_run:
            # recorded release is gone/retired or someone else's
            say(
                f".release-owner for {owner.get('branch', '?')} is stale "
                "(no matching live release) — discarded"
            )
            clear_owner()
        dep = rel.get("deployment") or {}
        dsha = str(dep.get("sha") or "")
        if mine and (why := stale_why(rel, prod)):
            # Ownership does not revive a corpse: an owned record prod
            # moved past is the same blocker a foreign one is (gr573151).
            return die(4, f"scripts/release: {why}")
        if (
            mine
            and not args.cut_only
            and dep.get("converged")
            and not dep.get("rollout_at")
        ):
            if args.dry_run:
                say("would finish the prod pointer and round without another rollout")
                return 0
            return deploy_and_close(args, dsha, finish_pointer=True)
        if (
            mine
            and not args.cut_only
            and dep.get("rollout_at")
            and not dep.get("runtime_confirmed_at")
            and rev(dsha)
            and ancestor(s, dsha)
        ):
            # prod already holds it, but the coordinator has not confirmed runtime
            say(f"resuming release {rel.get('branch')}: rollout done, {s9} pinned")
            say(next_line(dsha))
            return 3
        if prod and rev(prod) and ancestor(s, prod):
            say(f"already live: {s9} is in origin/prod {prod[:9]}")
            if mine and not args.cut_only and cur.get("open") and dsha == prod:
                if args.dry_run:
                    say("would finish the open round without another rollout")
                    return 0
                return deploy_and_close(args, dsha, finish_pointer=True)
            return 0

        branches = release_branches()
        if branches is None:
            return die(1, "scripts/release: cannot read origin's branches")
        # gr675506: the shared row adds as well as subtracts — a live row
        # naming a branch on origin is in flight whatever round.json says.
        for r in live_releases(cur, shared, branches):
            b = str(r.get("branch"))
            if not r.get("from_row") or b in {str(x.get("branch")) for x in live}:
                continue
            head = remote(f"refs/heads/{b}") or ""
            if head and not rev(head):
                git("fetch", "-q", "origin", f"refs/heads/{b}")
            if head and prod and rev(head) and rev(prod) and ancestor(head, prod):
                # A row stuck non-terminal on a release prod already holds
                # is a stale row, not in-flight work: the same retire rule
                # as an unrecorded branch below.
                say(
                    f"{b}: head on prod, shared row says {r.get('state')} — "
                    f"stale row ignored; delete with: git push origin --delete {b}"
                )
                continue
            live.append(r)
        recorded = {
            str(r["branch"]): str(r.get("state") or "")
            for r in cur.get("releases") or []
            if r.get("branch")
        }
        live_branches = {str(r["branch"]) for r in live}
        foreign = []
        for b in branches:
            if b in live_branches:
                continue
            head = remote(f"refs/heads/{b}") or ""
            if head and not rev(head):
                git("fetch", "-q", "origin", f"refs/heads/{b}")
            if b not in recorded and head and rev(head) and ancestor(s, head):
                # gr646224: round.json is per checkout — an unrecorded branch
                # already holding S is another checkout's cut of it, in flight
                # even with its head on main; cutting again gates S twice.
                say(
                    f"release in flight: {b} at {head[:9]} already holds {s9} "
                    "(cut by another checkout)"
                )
                foreign.append(b)
            elif head and prod and rev(head) and rev(prod) and ancestor(head, prod):
                # gr544044/gr675506: a leftover branch whose head origin/prod
                # contains (deployed) is retired — not in flight. A head
                # merely on main is NOT retired: every fresh cut is main's
                # tip, so another cutter's live release passes that test.
                # Deleting it is the operator's call, so name the command.
                say(
                    f"{b}: head on prod, {recorded.get(b) or 'stale'} — ignored; "
                    f"delete with: git push origin --delete {b}"
                )
            elif b not in recorded:
                foreign.append(b)

        if live or foreign:
            if (
                not mine
                and args.cut_only
                and not args.dry_run
                and open_rel
                and open_rel.get("cut_pending")
                and not remote(f"refs/heads/{open_rel['branch']}")
            ):
                # gr593418: a pending cut is this clone's record alone
                # (round.json is per checkout) — a hand-run `round cut`
                # whose push had no credentials leaves it without a
                # marker. No other session can own an unpushed branch, so
                # the dark pass adopts it and `round cut` finishes the push
                # at the recorded base instead of reporting it in flight.
                say(
                    f"adopting pending cut {open_rel['branch']} recorded in "
                    "this checkout"
                )
                write_owner(
                    str(open_rel["branch"]),
                    str(open_rel["base"]),
                    str(open_rel.get("cut_at") or ""),
                )
                rel, mine = open_rel, True
            if rel and mine:
                tip, has = head_containing(s, rel["branch"], rel.get("base", ""))
                return resume(args, s, rel, tip, has)
            for r in (open_rel, dep_rel):
                if not r or r.get("cut_pending"):
                    continue
                tip, has = head_containing(s, r["branch"], r.get("base", ""))
                if has:
                    say(f"riding release {r['branch']} at {tip[:9]}: it ships {s9}")
                    return 0
            for b in foreign + [str(r["branch"]) for r in live if r.get("from_row")]:
                tip, has = head_containing(s, b, "")
                if has:
                    say(f"riding release {b} at {tip[:9]}: it ships {s9}")
                    return 0
            # gr573151: a deploying/deployed record prod already moved past
            # is a dead clone's corpse, not in-flight work — but `round
            # deploy` would act on it before the next cut, so it blocks.
            # `round supersede` is the coordinator's call: refuse loudly.
            stale = [(r, why) for r in live if (why := stale_why(r, prod))]
            stale_ids = {id(r) for r, _ in stale}
            blockers = [
                str(r.get("branch") or f"r{r.get('n')}")
                for r in live
                if id(r) not in stale_ids
            ]
            blockers += foreign
            if blockers:
                for b in blockers:
                    say(f"release in flight: {b}")
                say(f"{s9} rides the next cut")
                return 0
            # No blockers means foreign was empty and every live record is
            # provably stale — `live or foreign` held, so stale names one.
            return die(4, f"scripts/release: {stale[0][1]}")

        # Nothing in flight: prepare the round, then cut. The next number is
        # global on origin (gr573151), not this clone's stale record. The
        # pairing guard gates only the selection of a cut (td695907): a
        # re-run resumes or reports a recorded release at its own base even
        # when the requested tip straddles an incomplete set. The snapshot
        # it checks is the one `round cut --main-at` must still see.
        main_at = rev("refs/remotes/origin/main")
        s, hold_why = _select_cut(s, main_at)
        if hold_why:
            return die(1, f"scripts/release: holding: {hold_why}")
        s9 = s[:9]
        n = int(cur.get("n", 0) or 0)
        is_open = bool(cur.get("open"))
        if is_open and cur.get("deployed"):
            if args.dry_run:
                nxt = next_round_n() or n + 1
                say(f"would close round {n} (deployed) and open round {nxt}")
                n = nxt
            else:
                for verb in ("close", "open"):
                    cp = rnd(verb)
                    sys.stdout.write(cp.stdout)
                    if cp.returncode:
                        sys.stderr.write(cp.stderr)
                        return 1
        elif not is_open:
            if args.dry_run:
                n = next_round_n() or n + 1
                say(f"would open round {n}")
            else:
                cp = rnd("open")
                sys.stdout.write(cp.stdout)
                if cp.returncode:
                    sys.stderr.write(cp.stderr)
                    return 1
        elif args.dry_run:
            n = next_round_n() or n
        if args.dry_run:
            return dry_run_plan(
                s, n, is_open and not cur.get("deployed"), cut_only=args.cut_only
            )

        rc = cut_and_finish(args, s, main_at=main_at)
        if rc in (-1, MAIN_MOVED):  # lost a cut race, or main moved: re-check
            races += 1
            if races > MAX_CUT_RACES:
                if rc == MAIN_MOVED:
                    # Heavy landing is transient: a classified wait, and the
                    # next pass re-checks on a fresh fetch (td759095).
                    return die(
                        1,
                        "scripts/release: holding: origin/main kept moving while "
                        f"the pairings were checked ({races} tries) — re-run",
                    )
                return die(1, "scripts/release: gave up after repeated cut races")
            continue
        return rc


def dry_run_plan(s: str, n: int, can_ask_cut: bool, *, cut_only: bool = False) -> int:
    if can_ask_cut:  # `round cut` refuses without an open round
        cp = rnd("cut", "--sha", s, "--dry-run")
        sys.stdout.write(cp.stdout)
        if cp.returncode:
            sys.stderr.write(cp.stderr)
    say(f"would cut release/r{n} at {s[:9]}")
    if cut_only:
        say("would stop after the cut (--cut-only)")
        return 0
    say("would wait for CI")
    say("would deploy")
    return 0


def report_cut(branch: str = "") -> int:
    """--cut-only's result: ``RELEASE_CUT: r<N> <head>`` and the verdict
    range (``<prev_prod>..<head>``, the same spelling note_ci_verdict
    records), then exit 0 without waiting for CI."""
    cur = round_state() or {}
    rel = (
        next((r for r in live_releases(cur) if r.get("branch") == branch), {})
        if branch
        else _release_by_state(cur, OPEN_STATES)
    )
    branch = str(rel.get("branch") or branch or f"release/r{cur.get('n')}")
    head = remote(f"refs/heads/{branch}")
    if head is None:  # origin unreadable: the record's base stands in
        head = str(rel.get("base") or "")
    if not head:
        # gr724696: absent on origin is not a cut — never the record's base.
        return die(1, f"scripts/release: {branch} has no head on origin after the cut")
    prev = rev("origin/prod")
    n = LANDED_SHA_LEN
    rng = f"{prev[:n]}..{head[:n]}" if prev else head
    say(f"RELEASE_CUT: {branch.rsplit('/', 1)[-1]} {head}")
    say(f"RELEASE_RANGE: {rng}")
    return 0


_DEPLOY_ONLY_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:release/)?r?(\d+)$")


def sync_to_release_head(want: str) -> str:
    """Fast-forward this checkout to ``origin/<want>`` (gr699137).

    A release that took a fix commit has a head past the cut sha this
    checkout sits on; once the rollout moves origin/prod there, every
    ``scripts/prod-precis`` write from the cut sha refuses ("not a
    descendant of origin/prod"). Best-effort: returns one status line, and
    a checkout that is not behind (or cannot fast-forward) is left alone.
    """
    spec = f"+refs/heads/{want}:refs/remotes/origin/{want}"
    if git("fetch", "-q", "origin", spec) is None:
        return f"checkout not moved: cannot fetch origin/{want}"
    tip = rev(f"origin/{want}")
    if not tip:
        return f"checkout not moved: no origin/{want}"
    if ancestor(tip, "HEAD"):
        return ""
    if not ancestor("HEAD", tip):
        return f"checkout not moved: HEAD is not an ancestor of origin/{want}"
    if git("merge", "--ff-only", tip) is None:
        return f"checkout not moved: cannot fast-forward to {tip[:9]}"
    return f"checkout moved to {want} head {tip[:9]}"


def deploy_only(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """--deploy-only r<N>: deploy the live recorded release/r<N> this
    worktree cut, pinned. Never cuts; any other state refuses with exit 1."""
    m = _DEPLOY_ONLY_RE.match(str(args.deploy_only).strip())
    if m is None:
        return die(
            1, f"scripts/release: --deploy-only {args.deploy_only!r} is not r<N>"
        )
    want = f"release/r{m.group(1)}"
    fetch()
    cur = round_state()
    if cur is None:
        return die(1, "scripts/release: cannot read `scripts/round status --json`")
    rel = next((r for r in live_releases(cur) if r.get("branch") == want), None)
    if rel is None:
        return die(
            1,
            f"scripts/release: {want} is not a live recorded release — nothing "
            "deployed; cut one with scripts/release --cut-only",
        )
    if rel.get("cut_pending"):
        return die(
            1, f"scripts/release: {want} has a pending cut — re-run --cut-only first"
        )
    if not owns(rel):
        if not args.adopt:
            return die(
                1,
                f"scripts/release: this worktree did not cut {want}; re-run with "
                "--adopt only if its cutting session died",
            )
        if not args.dry_run:
            write_owner(str(rel["branch"]), str(rel["base"]), str(rel.get("cut_at")))
    dep = rel.get("deployment") or {}
    sha = str(rel.get("base") or "")
    if not args.dry_run and (moved := sync_to_release_head(want)):
        say(moved)
    if dep.get("converged") and not dep.get("rollout_at"):
        if args.dry_run:
            say("would finish the prod pointer and round without another rollout")
            return 0
        return deploy_and_close(args, pending_sha(rel, sha), finish_pointer=True)
    if dep.get("rollout_at") and not dep.get("runtime_confirmed_at"):
        say(f"{want}: rollout already done")
        say(next_line(pending_sha(rel, sha)))
        return 3
    if args.dry_run:
        say(f"would deploy {want}")
        return 0
    if refusal := _factory_overlay_refusal(ROOT):
        # gr700433: refuse before the CI wait too — a lane whose overlay
        # cannot sync has nothing to wait for.
        return die(DEPLOY_RC_UNTOUCHED, refusal)
    facts = gate_facts()
    head = str(facts.get("release_head") or "")
    if not (
        head and facts.get("release_branch") == want and facts.get("candidate") == head
    ):
        rc = wait_ci(want, args)
        if rc:
            if not dep:
                # gr741486: the wait ended red or timed out with no deploy
                # leg ever recorded. release_deploy reads these words and
                # lets the release take fixes again.
                print(
                    f"scripts/release: {want} has no green head to deploy — "
                    "no host was touched",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                    flush=True,
                )
            return rc
    return deploy_and_close(args, sha)


def cut_and_finish(
    args: argparse.Namespace,
    s: str,
    branch: str = "",
    *,
    supersede: bool = False,
    main_at: str = "",
    require_pushed: bool = False,
) -> int:
    """Cut (or reconcile our own pending cut), wait for CI, deploy. -1 = not ours.

    ``supersede`` passes ``--supersede`` to ``round cut``: the caller has
    already proved the open release red and contained in main. ``main_at``
    passes ``--main-at``: the origin/main snapshot the caller checked the
    declared pairings against (gr687874); ``round cut`` pushes nothing
    when its own refresh of main differs, and this returns MAIN_MOVED.
    ``require_pushed`` passes ``--require-pushed``: the caller skipped the
    pairing check because origin held the pending branch, so round only
    finalizes it and never pushes it."""
    reconcile = bool(branch)  # the caller names the branch only for a pending cut
    if not branch:
        n = next_round_n() or (round_state() or {}).get("n")
        branch = f"release/r{n}"
    # gr675506: every cut path, a re-cut too, passes here — one line each.
    if warned := hand_cut_warning():
        print(f"scripts/release: {warned}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
    else:
        say(f"cutting under release pass {release_pass()}")
    prod = remote("refs/heads/prod") or ""
    if prod and not rev(prod):
        git("fetch", "-q", "origin", "prod")
    if prod and rev(prod) and ancestor(s, prod):
        # gr582590: a cut whose base prod already contains arms a rollback —
        # refuse before `round cut` runs or a marker claims the corpse.
        return die(
            1,
            f"scripts/release: the cut sha {s[:9]} is already on origin/prod "
            f"{prod[:9]} — cutting it would ship a rollback",
        )
    pre = read_owner()
    preowned = pre.get("branch") == branch and pre.get("base") == s
    stamp = datetime.now(UTC).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
    # Marker first: a crash between cut and marker must still resume as ours.
    write_owner(branch, s, stamp)
    cp = rnd(
        "cut",
        "--sha",
        s,
        *(("--supersede",) if supersede else ()),
        *(("--main-at", main_at) if main_at else ()),
        *(("--require-pushed",) if require_pushed else ()),
    )
    sys.stdout.write(cp.stdout)
    sys.stdout.flush()
    if cp.returncode:
        err = cp.stderr
        if _MAIN_MOVED_PHRASE in err:
            # Nothing pushed or recorded. A pending cut's intent stays ours.
            if not reconcile:
                clear_owner()
            sys.stderr.write(err)
            return MAIN_MOVED
        if "is already tagged on origin" in err:
            clear_owner()  # stale round number: nothing recorded by us
            return die(1, err.rstrip())
        if "exists on origin" in err or "already records an open release" in err:
            clear_owner()  # lost the race: nothing recorded by us
            sys.stderr.write(err)
            return -1
        # Anything else may leave our cut_pending intent recorded: keep ownership.
        return die(1, err.rstrip())
    fresh = f" cut at {s[:9]}" in cp.stdout
    if not (fresh or preowned or args.adopt):
        clear_owner()  # reconciled someone else's pending cut: not ours
        return -1
    rel = _release_by_state(round_state() or {}, OPEN_STATES)
    if rel:
        write_owner(str(rel["branch"]), str(rel["base"]), str(rel.get("cut_at", "")))
    return finish(args, s)


def finish(args: argparse.Namespace, s: str) -> int:
    cur = round_state() or {}
    rel = _release_by_state(cur, OPEN_STATES)
    branch = str(rel.get("branch") or f"release/r{cur.get('n')}")
    if getattr(args, "cut_only", False):
        return report_cut(branch)
    rc = wait_ci(branch, args)
    if rc:
        return rc
    return deploy_and_close(args, s)


def resume(
    args: argparse.Namespace, s: str, rel: dict[str, Any], tip: str, has: bool
) -> int:
    """Act on our own release first; S not in it rides the next release."""
    branch = str(rel["branch"])
    dep = rel.get("deployment") or {}
    if rel.get("cut_pending"):
        if getattr(args, "sha", "") and not has:
            # gr724696: the pending cut pins another sha — reconciling it
            # would push a branch the caller cannot record as its cut.
            say(
                f"release in flight: {branch} (a pending cut at "
                f"{str(rel.get('base'))[:9]}; drop it with `scripts/round cut "
                "--abandon`)"
            )
            say(f"{s[:9]} rides the next cut")
            return 0
        say(f"resuming: {branch} has a pending cut — reconciling")
        base = str(rel["base"])
        main_at = ""
        pushed = remote(f"refs/heads/{branch}") == base
        if pushed:
            # td759095: origin already holds the branch at the recorded base
            # (the push landed, the record was never finalized). Reconciling
            # publishes nothing — round only finalizes the record — so the
            # pairing guard does not apply: the cut was checked when it was
            # pushed, and a counterpart landed since rides the next cut.
            # --require-pushed: a branch deleted before round reads it is
            # never re-pushed unchecked.
            say(f"{branch} is already on origin at {base[:9]} — finalizing")
        else:
            # Reconciling would push the branch (an unreadable or different
            # remote, round refuses): it is a cut publication like any
            # other, so check the pairings and pin the snapshot.
            main_at = rev("refs/remotes/origin/main")
            safe, hold_why = _split_set_cut(base, main_at)
            if hold_why or safe != base:
                why = hold_why or f"the safe cut is {safe[:9]}"
                return die(
                    1,
                    f"scripts/release: holding: pending cut at {base[:9]}: {why}. "
                    "Run scripts/round cut --abandon before cutting again.",
                )
        if args.dry_run:
            say("would reconcile the cut, wait for CI, deploy")
            return 0
        # the recorded base, not S: cut refuses a different pending SHA
        rc = cut_and_finish(args, base, branch, main_at=main_at, require_pushed=pushed)
        if rc == MAIN_MOVED:
            return die(
                1,
                f"scripts/release: holding: pending cut at {base[:9]}: "
                "origin/main moved while its pairings were checked — re-run",
            )
        return die(1, "scripts/release: lost the pending cut") if rc == -1 else rc
    if has:
        say(f"resuming release {branch} at {tip[:9]}: it ships {s[:9]}")
    else:
        say(
            f"resuming release {branch} at {tip[:9]}: {s[:9]} is not in it "
            "and rides the next release"
        )
    if getattr(args, "cut_only", False):
        if (
            not has
            and rel.get("state") in OPEN_STATES
            and not rel.get("cut_pending")
            and tip
            and ancestor(tip, "refs/remotes/origin/main")
            and isinstance(ci_red(tip), dict)
        ):
            # gr573151: our release went red and its head is fully on main
            # (the fix already landed) — re-cut at S instead of reporting
            # the dead cut; `round cut` supersedes it in the record.
            # gr648267: pass --supersede — round's own red reader needs
            # every gate leg completed and reads a rerun in flight as
            # undecided, so its auto-supersede would refuse the cut.
            main_at = rev("refs/remotes/origin/main")
            s, hold_why = _select_cut(s, main_at)
            if hold_why:
                return die(1, f"scripts/release: holding: {hold_why}")
            say(
                f"release {branch} is red and its head is on main: "
                f"re-cutting at {s[:9]}"
            )
            if args.dry_run:
                say(f"would mark {branch} superseded and cut at {s[:9]}")
                return 0
            rc = cut_and_finish(args, s, supersede=True, main_at=main_at)
            if rc:
                # Any non-zero outcome can leave the marker naming a branch
                # that was never recorded (the refusal) or cleared (a lost
                # race): put ours back while the red record is still live,
                # else the next run reads it as a foreign release in
                # flight. A pending re-cut keeps its r<N+1> marker — the
                # terminal record must not reclaim it and orphan the intent.
                still = next(
                    (
                        r
                        for r in live_releases(round_state() or {})
                        if r.get("branch") == branch
                    ),
                    None,
                )
                if still is not None:
                    write_owner(
                        str(still["branch"]),
                        str(still["base"]),
                        str(still.get("cut_at") or ""),
                    )
                if rc == MAIN_MOVED:
                    return die(
                        1,
                        f"scripts/release: holding: re-cut of {branch}: origin/main "
                        "moved while its pairings were checked — re-run",
                    )
                if rc == -1:
                    return die(
                        1,
                        f"scripts/release: re-cut of {branch} refused — see above",
                    )
            return rc
        if not has and (
            getattr(args, "sha", "") or rel.get("state") not in OPEN_STATES
        ):
            # gr724696: RELEASE_CUT names the release that ships S. A caller
            # that asked for S by sha, or a record already past its cut
            # (deploying, deployed, a failed partial rollout), must not be
            # handed another release's head as its cut — r333's old base was
            # gated and nearly deployed that way. Only the no-sha re-run on
            # this tree's own open release reports it (below).
            say(f"release in flight: {branch}")
            say(f"{s[:9]} rides the next cut")
            return 0
        return 0 if args.dry_run else report_cut(branch)
    if dep.get("converged") and not dep.get("rollout_at"):
        if args.dry_run:
            say("would finish the prod pointer and round without another rollout")
            return 0
        return deploy_and_close(args, pending_sha(rel, s), finish_pointer=True)
    if dep.get("rollout_at") and not dep.get("runtime_confirmed_at"):
        say(next_line(pending_sha(rel, s)))
        return 3
    if args.dry_run:
        say("would wait for CI")
        rc = rnd("deploy", "--dry-run", stream=True).returncode
        if rc:
            say(f"(round deploy --dry-run: exit {rc} — expected until CI is green)")
        return 0
    facts = gate_facts()
    head = str(facts.get("release_head") or "")
    ours = facts.get("release_branch") == branch
    if not (head and ours and facts.get("candidate") == head):
        rc = wait_ci(branch, args)
        if rc:
            return rc
    return deploy_and_close(args, s)


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="scripts/release",
        description="Cut a release at main SHA S, wait for CI, deploy it.",
    )
    ap.add_argument("--sha", default="", help="commit on origin/main (default: tip)")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--queue-timeout",
        type=float,
        default=0.0,
        metavar="MIN",
        help=argparse.SUPPRESS,  # removed: step 3 no longer queues (release-train §5)
    )
    ap.add_argument("--ci-timeout", type=float, default=90.0, metavar="MIN")
    ap.add_argument("--poll", type=float, default=60.0, metavar="SEC")
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--adopt",
        action="store_true",
        help="take ownership of the recorded release (its cutting session died)",
    )
    mode = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument(
        "--cut-only",
        action="store_true",
        help="cut (or report this worktree's open release) and exit without "
        "waiting for CI; prints RELEASE_CUT: r<N> <sha>",
    )
    mode.add_argument(
        "--deploy-only",
        default="",
        metavar="rN",
        help="deploy the recorded release/r<N> this worktree cut; never cuts",
    )
    mode.add_argument(
        "--record-prod-head",
        default="",
        metavar="SHA",
        help="only write prod's release.prod_head record for deployed SHA "
        "(scripts/deploy calls this after moving origin/prod)",
    )
    return run(ap.parse_args(argv))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
